  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You send the wrong version of a document to a teammate and notice it ten minutes later. Other people have already planned around the commitment you made. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell them immediately, send the correct version, and explain which file to use$qb$, $qb$Send the correct version with a short correction message$qb$, $qb$Send the correct version without mentioning the mistake$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether they use the wrong file$qb$, $qb$Delete your copy of the wrong version$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have several urgent tasks and notice you are starting to skip checks. Several urgent requests are competing for the same limited attention. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Reprioritize, protect the essential checks, and communicate any realistic delay$qb$, $qb$Slow down enough to complete the most important checks$qb$, $qb$Continue quickly and review everything at the end$qb$, $qb$Skip checks on smaller tasks$qb$, $qb$Keep the same pace and hope no errors occur$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague seems unusually quiet after their idea is rejected. The conversation may affect how willing the colleague is to work with you afterward. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check in privately and give them space to explain how they see the situation$qb$, $qb$Ask whether they want to discuss what happened$qb$, $qb$Tell them rejection is normal at work$qb$, $qb$Avoid the topic so they do not feel embarrassed$qb$, $qb$Tell the team to be extra nice to them$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You receive the same improvement suggestion from two different people. The lesson should improve future work, not only correct this one case. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Treat it as useful evidence, test a change, and check whether the result improves$qb$, $qb$Compare the two examples and adjust your next attempt$qb$, $qb$Thank both people but keep your current approach$qb$, $qb$Wait for a manager to give the same feedback$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because neither person is your manager$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A shared checklist has one obvious outdated link and you know the correct link. The issue is small now but appears often enough to create repeated waste. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Update it through the proper process and tell the owner what changed$qb$, $qb$Send the correct link to the owner and offer to update it$qb$, $qb$Use the correct link only for your own work$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone reports the broken link$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because you do not own the checklist$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You own a project risk that has increased, but earlier updates described it as low. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Update the risk honestly, explain what changed, assess the impact, and lead the revised mitigation rather than defending the earlier rating$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the risk has increased and propose stronger mitigation$qb$, $qb$Keep the old rating until the impact becomes clearer$qb$, $qb$Explain that the earlier rating was reasonable and leave ownership with the risk source$qb$, $qb$Wait for a manager to change the rating$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A sudden workload surge is causing the team to rush and error rates are rising. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Triage by impact, reduce or sequence lower-value work, protect critical controls, and communicate capacity limits early$qb$, $qb$Identify the highest-risk activities and temporarily reduce lower-priority commitments$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to work faster while checking each other's work$qb$, $qb$Keep all commitments and correct mistakes later$qb$, $qb$Relax quality checks until the surge is over$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague who usually supports your work becomes distant after being excluded from an important discussion. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the exclusion, invite their perspective, understand the impact, and rebuild involvement where it still matters$qb$, $qb$Ask how the earlier decision affected them and what would improve collaboration now$qb$, $qb$Explain that the discussion had to happen quickly$qb$, $qb$Ignore the distance and focus on the work$qb$, $qb$Tell them they need to separate feelings from work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A technique that improved your own output creates extra checking work for another team. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Study the downstream effect, adapt the technique, and test whether the revised method improves the overall result$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team for examples and adjust the technique where needed$qb$, $qb$Keep the method because your own work is better$qb$, $qb$Stop using the technique completely$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team to streamline their checking$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R4-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You notice a recurring coordination problem that wastes time across two teams but has no clear owner. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Validate the pattern, bring the right people together, and propose a practical ownership and process improvement$qb$, $qb$Collect examples and raise the pattern with both teams$qb$, $qb$Create a workaround for your own tasks$qb$, $qb$Wait for senior management to assign ownership$qb$, $qb$Continue solving each case individually$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You realize your late reply caused someone else to miss a small deadline. Other people have already planned around the commitment you made. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the delay, help recover the impact, and change how you track such requests$qb$, $qb$Apologize, respond now, and agree the next step$qb$, $qb$Explain that you had other priorities$qb$, $qb$Say the missed deadline was minor$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the deadline has passed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are under time pressure and two sources show different figures. Speed matters, but errors would create a larger operational problem. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Resolve or clearly flag the difference before using the figure, even if the update takes slightly longer$qb$, $qb$Check the more reliable source and explain any remaining uncertainty$qb$, $qb$Choose the higher figure to be safe$qb$, $qb$Use whichever figure is easier to access$qb$, $qb$Average the figures without checking$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague makes a short, unusually sharp reply during a stressful day. The working relationship will continue after the immediate issue. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Avoid assuming intent, stay calm, and check whether there is an issue that needs attention$qb$, $qb$Respond calmly and ask whether everything is okay$qb$, $qb$Tell them their tone is unacceptable immediately$qb$, $qb$Reply sharply so they understand the impact$qb$, $qb$Complain about them to another colleague$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You complete a task faster than before but make one new error. The lesson should change future behaviour, not only repair this case. What is the best learning response?$qb$, $qb$Keep the useful speed improvement, understand the error, and adjust the method before repeating it$qb$, $qb$Review what caused the error and modify the faster method$qb$, $qb$Return completely to the old slower method$qb$, $qb$Ignore the error because overall speed improved$qb$, $qb$Keep the faster method unchanged$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R6-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You notice a small backlog building in an area you can help with after your priority work is complete. The pattern is recurring across more than one person. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check what help is useful, take an appropriate part, and keep the owner informed$qb$, $qb$Offer to help with a defined part of the backlog$qb$, $qb$Start working on random backlog items$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone assigns you work$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the backlog belongs to another person$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You gave a colleague an incorrect date and they planned work around it. Other people have planned around your commitment. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the date immediately, acknowledge the impact, and help adjust the plan$qb$, $qb$Tell them the correct date and apologize$qb$, $qb$Update the date in the system without contacting them$qb$, $qb$Wait until they notice the change$qb$, $qb$Say the original date was only an estimate$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
