  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague becomes defensive when you ask about a missed task. The colleague's willingness to cooperate later may depend on how you respond now. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Lower the tension, ask what happened, and listen before discussing the next step$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge that the conversation may feel difficult and ask for the facts$qb$, $qb$Repeat that the task was missed$qb$, $qb$Tell them defensiveness is unhelpful$qb$, $qb$End the discussion$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new task goes badly even though you prepared carefully. The lesson should improve future performance, not only fix the current case. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Review what happened, identify one or two lessons, and apply them to the next attempt$qb$, $qb$Ask for feedback and note what you would change next time$qb$, $qb$Avoid the task until you have more experience$qb$, $qb$Conclude that the task is not suited to you$qb$, $qb$Repeat the same approach because preparation was good$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R7-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You notice that a recurring team reminder is no longer being sent because its owner left. The issue is recurring across several tasks rather than appearing once. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the reminder is still needed and help restore clear ownership$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the reminder has stopped and ask who should own it$qb$, $qb$Send the reminder yourself once$qb$, $qb$Wait for a manager to notice$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the former owner left$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You realize a task you own may miss its deadline. Other people have already planned around your commitment. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Raise the risk early, explain the cause, and propose a recovery plan$qb$, $qb$Tell the person waiting for it that there may be a delay$qb$, $qb$Work longer without telling anyone and hope to recover$qb$, $qb$Wait until the deadline before explaining the problem$qb$, $qb$Blame the dependency that caused the delay$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R8-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Three urgent requests arrive while you are finishing an important check. Speed matters, but errors would create larger downstream consequences. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Prioritize by impact and deadline, then communicate what you can handle$qb$, $qb$Finish the important check before reviewing the new requests$qb$, $qb$Start the newest request because it arrived most recently$qb$, $qb$Try to work on all four tasks at the same time$qb$, $qb$Rush the check so you can clear the other requests$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
