  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague becomes defensive when you ask about a missed task. The colleague's response may reflect loss of status, trust, or control that could undermine a wider change. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Lower the tension, ask what happened, and listen before discussing the next step$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge that the conversation may feel difficult and ask for the facts$qb$, $qb$Repeat that the task was missed$qb$, $qb$Tell them defensiveness is unhelpful$qb$, $qb$End the discussion$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new task goes badly even though you prepared carefully. The learning has implications for standards and practices beyond your own work. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Review what happened, identify one or two lessons, and apply them to the next attempt$qb$, $qb$Ask for feedback and note what you would change next time$qb$, $qb$Avoid the task until you have more experience$qb$, $qb$Conclude that the task is not suited to you$qb$, $qb$Repeat the same approach because preparation was good$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You notice that a recurring team reminder is no longer being sent because its owner left. The issue spans functions, recurs at scale, and has no clear end-to-end owner. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the reminder is still needed and help restore clear ownership$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the reminder has stopped and ask who should own it$qb$, $qb$Send the reminder yourself once$qb$, $qb$Wait for a manager to notice$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the former owner left$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You realize your late reply caused someone else to miss a small deadline. The missed commitment now affects several teams and requires visible recovery leadership rather than a local correction. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the delay, help recover the impact, and change how you track such requests$qb$, $qb$Apologize, respond now, and agree the next step$qb$, $qb$Explain that you had other priorities$qb$, $qb$Say the missed deadline was minor$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the deadline has passed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are under time pressure and two sources show different figures. The pressure is prolonged, error rates are rising, and continued overload creates operational and people risk. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Resolve or clearly flag the difference before using the figure, even if the update takes slightly longer$qb$, $qb$Check the more reliable source and explain any remaining uncertainty$qb$, $qb$Choose the higher figure to be safe$qb$, $qb$Use whichever figure is easier to access$qb$, $qb$Average the figures without checking$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague makes a short, unusually sharp reply during a stressful day. The colleague's reaction may reflect loss of status, trust, or influence that could undermine a wider change. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Avoid assuming intent, stay calm, and check whether there is an issue that needs attention$qb$, $qb$Respond calmly and ask whether everything is okay$qb$, $qb$Tell them their tone is unacceptable immediately$qb$, $qb$Reply sharply so they understand the impact$qb$, $qb$Complain about them to another colleague$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You complete a task faster than before but make one new error. The learning has implications for standards and practices beyond your own area. What is the best learning response?$qb$, $qb$Keep the useful speed improvement, understand the error, and adjust the method before repeating it$qb$, $qb$Review what caused the error and modify the faster method$qb$, $qb$Return completely to the old slower method$qb$, $qb$Ignore the error because overall speed improved$qb$, $qb$Keep the faster method unchanged$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You notice a small backlog building in an area you can help with after your priority work is complete. The issue spans functions, recurs at scale, and lacks clear end-to-end accountability. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check what help is useful, take an appropriate part, and keep the owner informed$qb$, $qb$Offer to help with a defined part of the backlog$qb$, $qb$Start working on random backlog items$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone assigns you work$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the backlog belongs to another person$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The missed commitment now affects several teams, and recovery requires visible ownership rather than explanation alone. What demonstrates true end-to-end ownership when an automated script error causes missing client record entries?$qb$, $qb$Internalizing personal accountability, notifying stakeholders transparently, fixing bugs, and reconciling records.$qb$, $qb$Fixing the script error quietly and manually entering missing records before external stakeholders notice.$qb$, $qb$Reporting the system glitch to technical support and requesting that they restore missing records from backups.$qb$, $qb$Informing your lead that technical tool glitches caused record gaps and seeking guidance on next steps.$qb$, $qb$Waiting for finance teams to flag record discrepancies during monthly reconciliation before taking action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Overload has persisted for weeks; errors, fatigue, and dependency on a few individuals are all increasing. What is the most resilient response when a system outage occurs 1 hour prior to a major client submission deadline?$qb$, $qb$Disseminating clear status updates to clients with revised timelines while focusing on recovery coordination.$qb$, $qb$Answering incoming client messages individually as quickly as possible while technical teams work on recovery.$qb$, $qb$Focusing exclusively on technical recovery steps while deferring client communications until systems restore.$qb$, $qb$Forwarding client inquiries to department leadership so executive managers handle crisis communications.$qb$, $qb$Enabling automated out-of-office email replies informing clients that technical issues are being addressed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The colleague's resistance reflects both personal loss and a legitimate concern about how the wider change is being implemented. How should an employee apply emotional intelligence when a reliable peer demonstrates uncharacteristic quality drops?$qb$, $qb$Initiating a private, supportive check-in to express appreciation, understand challenges, and offer assistance.$qb$, $qb$Mentioning missed details quietly during routine work syncs to help the peer notice quality gaps.$qb$, $qb$Correcting the peer's work handoffs independently to maintain team deliverable quality without confrontation.$qb$, $qb$Informing the team lead about quality drops so workload can be reassigned through official channels.$qb$, $qb$Giving the peer space for several weeks, assuming work performance will self-correct over time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The lesson affects standards used by several teams, not just the method used in your own work. What reaction reflects a growth mindset when receiving critical stakeholder feedback on a submitted report?$qb$, $qb$Refining the analytical framework constructively, seeking specific clarity, and resubmitting enhanced work.$qb$, $qb$Thanking the stakeholder for feedback and applying requested line-item edits to the report.$qb$, $qb$Explaining time constraints to the stakeholder to contextualize why initial analytical depth was limited.$qb$, $qb$Requesting a senior peer to review updated report drafts before resubmitting work to stakeholders.$qb$, $qb$Accepting feedback quietly and relying strictly on standard report templates for future assignments.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The recurring issue crosses functions, survives local fixes, and no leader currently owns the end-to-end outcome. How can an associate best demonstrate enterprise during low-volume operational periods?$qb$, $qb$Pitching unprompted workflow enhancements after auditing internal documentation and identifying team bottlenecks.$qb$, $qb$Reaching out to team members casually to check if they require help with ongoing daily tasks.$qb$, $qb$Completing optional online learning modules available on the corporate training portal.$qb$, $qb$Organizing personal email folders and digital documents to prepare for upcoming workload cycles.$qb$, $qb$Remaining available on communication channels while awaiting new task assignments from supervisors.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You realize a task you own may miss its deadline. The failure now affects several teams, and your earlier assurance influenced how they planned. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Raise the risk early, explain the cause, and propose a recovery plan$qb$, $qb$Tell the person waiting for it that there may be a delay$qb$, $qb$Work longer without telling anyone and hope to recover$qb$, $qb$Wait until the deadline before explaining the problem$qb$, $qb$Blame the dependency that caused the delay$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Three urgent requests arrive while you are finishing an important check. High demand has persisted; errors, fatigue, and dependence on a few key people are all rising. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Prioritize by impact and deadline, then communicate what you can handle$qb$, $qb$Finish the important check before reviewing the new requests$qb$, $qb$Start the newest request because it arrived most recently$qb$, $qb$Try to work on all four tasks at the same time$qb$, $qb$Rush the check so you can clear the other requests$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague who is usually reliable seems upset and makes an unusual mistake. The colleague's resistance may reflect loss of influence and a valid concern about the wider change design. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check in privately and ask whether they need support$qb$, $qb$Ask what happened before discussing the mistake$qb$, $qb$Correct the mistake yourself and say nothing$qb$, $qb$Tell them they need to focus more carefully$qb$, $qb$Discuss their behaviour with other teammates$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You receive feedback that your reports are hard to scan quickly. The learning could require changes to standards, coaching, and practices across more than your own team. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask for an example, change the format, and check whether it improves$qb$, $qb$Try a clearer format in your next report$qb$, $qb$Explain why the current format contains useful detail$qb$, $qb$Wait for the same feedback from another person$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the reports are technically correct$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You finish your assigned work early and notice a small issue that may affect tomorrow's task. The recurring issue crosses functions, survives local fixes, and has no clear end-to-end owner. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Investigate the issue and take a useful next step within your role$qb$, $qb$Flag the issue and offer to help resolve it$qb$, $qb$Wait for tomorrow because today's work is complete$qb$, $qb$Leave it for the person assigned tomorrow$qb$, $qb$Ignore it unless it becomes a larger problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague tells you that your handoff was incomplete and delayed their work. The failure now affects several teams, and your earlier assurance influenced how they planned. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the gap, complete the handoff, and improve your checklist$qb$, $qb$Complete the missing part and apologize for the delay$qb$, $qb$Explain that the handoff format was not clearly defined$qb$, $qb$Ask what they could have done to spot the gap earlier$qb$, $qb$Say the delay was small and move on$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A senior person asks for an urgent update while you are handling another urgent issue. High demand has persisted; errors, fatigue, and dependency on key individuals are increasing. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$State what you are handling, confirm priorities, and give a realistic update time$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the request and provide the best timing you can$qb$, $qb$Drop the current issue immediately because the requester is senior$qb$, $qb$Try to answer both requests at once$qb$, $qb$Promise an immediate update even if you cannot deliver it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate becomes quiet after receiving critical feedback. The colleague's resistance reflects both personal loss and a credible concern about the wider change design. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check in privately and listen before offering advice$qb$, $qb$Ask whether they want to talk about the feedback$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to take the feedback personally$qb$, $qb$Give them space and avoid the topic completely$qb$, $qb$Tell others they seem upset so the team can help$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You make the same small error twice in one month. The lesson could require changes to standards, coaching, and operating practices across several teams. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Identify why it repeated and change your working method$qb$, $qb$Review both cases and add a check to your routine$qb$, $qb$Be more careful next time$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to check all your work$qb$, $qb$Accept that occasional errors are normal$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A useful team document is outdated, and nobody has been assigned to update it. The recurring issue spans functions, survives local fixes, and lacks clear end-to-end accountability. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the need and update it within your role, then share the change$qb$, $qb$Flag the outdated section and offer to update it$qb$, $qb$Wait for the document owner to notice$qb$, $qb$Create a separate personal version$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because it is not assigned to you$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You send the wrong version of a document to a teammate and notice it ten minutes later. The failure now affects several teams, and your earlier assurance influenced their plans. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell them immediately, send the correct version, and explain which file to use$qb$, $qb$Send the correct version with a short correction message$qb$, $qb$Send the correct version without mentioning the mistake$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether they use the wrong file$qb$, $qb$Delete your copy of the wrong version$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have several urgent tasks and notice you are starting to skip checks. High demand has persisted; errors, fatigue, and dependency on key individuals are all increasing. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Reprioritize, protect the essential checks, and communicate any realistic delay$qb$, $qb$Slow down enough to complete the most important checks$qb$, $qb$Continue quickly and review everything at the end$qb$, $qb$Skip checks on smaller tasks$qb$, $qb$Keep the same pace and hope no errors occur$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague seems unusually quiet after their idea is rejected. The colleague's resistance reflects both personal loss and a credible concern about the wider change design. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check in privately and give them space to explain how they see the situation$qb$, $qb$Ask whether they want to discuss what happened$qb$, $qb$Tell them rejection is normal at work$qb$, $qb$Avoid the topic so they do not feel embarrassed$qb$, $qb$Tell the team to be extra nice to them$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You receive the same improvement suggestion from two different people. The lesson could require changes to standards, coaching, and operating practices across multiple teams. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Treat it as useful evidence, test a change, and check whether the result improves$qb$, $qb$Compare the two examples and adjust your next attempt$qb$, $qb$Thank both people but keep your current approach$qb$, $qb$Wait for a manager to give the same feedback$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because neither person is your manager$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A shared checklist has one obvious outdated link and you know the correct link. The recurring issue spans functions, survives local fixes, and lacks clear end-to-end accountability. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Update it through the proper process and tell the owner what changed$qb$, $qb$Send the correct link to the owner and offer to update it$qb$, $qb$Use the correct link only for your own work$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone reports the broken link$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because you do not own the checklist$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You realize you misunderstood an instruction and completed the wrong version of a task. The failure now affects several teams, and your earlier assurance influenced their plans. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Own the misunderstanding, clarify the requirement, and correct the work promptly$qb$, $qb$Tell the requester what happened and agree the correction$qb$, $qb$Correct the work without mentioning the misunderstanding$qb$, $qb$Explain that the instruction was unclear$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether the wrong version is accepted$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have a heavy workload and one task suddenly becomes more urgent. High demand has persisted; errors, fatigue, and dependency on key individuals are all increasing. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Reorder priorities, protect critical quality checks, and communicate any changed timing$qb$, $qb$Move the urgent task first and update people affected by the change$qb$, $qb$Work faster on everything$qb$, $qb$Add the urgent task without changing the plan$qb$, $qb$Skip checks so all tasks stay on time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague says they are struggling with workload but continues meeting deadlines. The colleague's resistance reflects both personal loss and a credible concern about the wider change design. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Listen, ask what is creating the pressure, and explore practical support$qb$, $qb$Ask which part of the workload is hardest$qb$, $qb$Tell them they seem to be managing well$qb$, $qb$Suggest they simply organize their time better$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because deadlines are being met$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You receive a correction on a task you have done many times. The lesson could require changes to standards, coaching, and operating practices across multiple teams. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Understand why the correction is needed and update your future approach$qb$, $qb$Ask for the reason and apply the correction next time$qb$, $qb$Make the correction only on this task$qb$, $qb$Defend your usual method because it worked before$qb$, $qb$Ignore the correction unless a manager repeats it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A small customer question is sitting unassigned and you know the answer. The recurring issue spans functions, survives local fixes, and lacks clear end-to-end accountability. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Answer it if it is within your role and make sure the request is properly recorded$qb$, $qb$Take ownership of the question and notify the usual owner$qb$, $qb$Wait for someone to assign it to you$qb$, $qb$Answer privately without updating the system$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because it is unassigned$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You tell a teammate a task is complete, then discover you missed one required check. The failure now affects several teams, and your earlier assurance influenced their plans. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell them immediately, complete the check, and correct any impact$qb$, $qb$Complete the check and update them if anything changes$qb$, $qb$Do the check quietly and say nothing if it passes$qb$, $qb$Wait until they use the work$qb$, $qb$Assume the check is unnecessary because the task looked correct$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
