  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You learn that a method you use is producing more errors than a newer method. The lesson could require changes to standards, coaching, and operating practices across multiple teams. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Understand the newer method, practice it, and compare your results$qb$, $qb$Try the newer method on suitable tasks$qb$, $qb$Keep your method because you are faster with it$qb$, $qb$Use the new method only when someone checks$qb$, $qb$Ignore the evidence because your method usually works$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You notice a recurring question from customers that could be answered by a short guide. The recurring issue spans functions, survives local fixes, and lacks clear end-to-end accountability. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the need, create a simple guide within your role, and have it checked before sharing$qb$, $qb$Suggest a guide and offer to draft it$qb$, $qb$Answer each customer separately$qb$, $qb$Wait for a manager to request a guide$qb$, $qb$Ignore the pattern because answering questions is part of the job$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You realize your late reply caused someone else to miss a small deadline. The failure now affects several teams, and your earlier assurance influenced their plans. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the delay, help recover the impact, and change how you track such requests$qb$, $qb$Apologize, respond now, and agree the next step$qb$, $qb$Explain that you had other priorities$qb$, $qb$Say the missed deadline was minor$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the deadline has passed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are under time pressure and two sources show different figures. High demand has persisted; errors, fatigue, and dependency on key individuals are all increasing. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Resolve or clearly flag the difference before using the figure, even if the update takes slightly longer$qb$, $qb$Check the more reliable source and explain any remaining uncertainty$qb$, $qb$Choose the higher figure to be safe$qb$, $qb$Use whichever figure is easier to access$qb$, $qb$Average the figures without checking$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague makes a short, unusually sharp reply during a stressful day. The reaction reflects both personal loss and a credible concern about the wider change design. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Avoid assuming intent, stay calm, and check whether there is an issue that needs attention$qb$, $qb$Respond calmly and ask whether everything is okay$qb$, $qb$Tell them their tone is unacceptable immediately$qb$, $qb$Reply sharply so they understand the impact$qb$, $qb$Complain about them to another colleague$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You complete a task faster than before but make one new error. The lesson could require changes to standards, coaching, and operating practices across multiple teams. What is the best learning response?$qb$, $qb$Keep the useful speed improvement, understand the error, and adjust the method before repeating it$qb$, $qb$Review what caused the error and modify the faster method$qb$, $qb$Return completely to the old slower method$qb$, $qb$Ignore the error because overall speed improved$qb$, $qb$Keep the faster method unchanged$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You notice a small backlog building in an area you can help with after your priority work is complete. The recurring issue spans functions, survives local fixes, and lacks clear end-to-end accountability. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Check what help is useful, take an appropriate part, and keep the owner informed$qb$, $qb$Offer to help with a defined part of the backlog$qb$, $qb$Start working on random backlog items$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone assigns you work$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the backlog belongs to another person$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A cross-functional decision you chaired creates an unexpected cost in another division. The information was available but not surfaced during the meeting. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Own the decision process, correct the impact, and change the decision mechanism so material cross-division effects are surfaced earlier.$qb$, $qb$Explain that the cost was not raised by the affected division and therefore was not reasonably foreseeable.$qb$, $qb$Ask the affected division to absorb the cost this time while the process is improved.$qb$, $qb$Reopen the decision only if the cost exceeds the division's tolerance.$qb$, $qb$Shift accountability to the person who held the information but failed to mention it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A cyber incident is unresolved after 18 hours. The team is fatigued, executives want faster recovery, and a risky shortcut could restore service sooner. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Maintain clear risk thresholds, rotate exhausted roles, protect critical thinking, and use the shortcut only if an authorized owner accepts the residual risk.$qb$, $qb$Take the shortcut because prolonged outage risk is now more important than procedural risk.$qb$, $qb$Continue the current plan with the same core team because changing people may slow recovery.$qb$, $qb$Pause recovery until the team has rested sufficiently.$qb$, $qb$Delegate the shortcut decision entirely to the most senior engineer on the call.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A manager who normally performs well has become abrupt with colleagues after a family crisis. Delivery is slipping and the team is beginning to react. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Address the behavior and impact privately with empathy, understand what support is needed, and agree near-term expectations that protect both the person and team.$qb$, $qb$Give the manager temporary flexibility and avoid discussing behavior until the crisis passes.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team to be patient because the manager is dealing with personal difficulty.$qb$, $qb$Move the manager's responsibilities to others without discussing the reason.$qb$, $qb$Start formal disciplinary action immediately because personal circumstances should not affect work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your function has adopted a new analytical method, but senior staff attend training and then return to old practices. What is most likely to create real learning?$qb$, $qb$Require application to live decisions, compare outcomes with prior methods, use peer review, and adapt the method based on evidence.$qb$, $qb$Make the training mandatory and test understanding at the end.$qb$, $qb$Ask senior staff to coach junior employees on the new method.$qb$, $qb$Circulate case studies showing why the new method is superior.$qb$, $qb$Stop allowing the old method immediately, regardless of context.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You see a recurring customer issue that sits outside your formal remit but could damage renewal revenue across several accounts. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Validate the pattern, quantify the business effect, convene the relevant owners, and drive a clear path to accountable action.$qb$, $qb$Inform the customer-success leader and ask them to investigate.$qb$, $qb$Fix the cases you personally encounter and track whether the pattern continues.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the issue to appear in formal churn reporting.$qb$, $qb$Avoid involvement because another function owns customer retention.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A portfolio you lead missed a public commitment because you approved competing priorities that overloaded a shared team. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Own the prioritization failure, stabilize commitments, reset capacity and decision rules, and communicate what will change at portfolio level.$qb$, $qb$Ask the shared team why it did not flag overload earlier.$qb$, $qb$Apologize and ask project managers to recover their schedules.$qb$, $qb$Replace the planning tool with one showing resource conflicts.$qb$, $qb$Attribute the miss to unusually high demand across projects.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$During a cyber incident, customer data may be exposed but confirmation could take hours. Disclosure has legal and reputational consequences. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Run disciplined incident command: protect containment, distinguish facts from hypotheses, use predefined disclosure thresholds, and update decision owners as evidence changes.$qb$, $qb$Wait for technical confirmation before involving executives.$qb$, $qb$Notify all customers immediately because transparency is safest.$qb$, $qb$Personally join the technical investigation to judge evidence.$qb$, $qb$Restore systems first and address disclosure afterward.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A senior leader who delivers strong results becomes dismissive and harsh after months of pressure, and team confidence is falling. What should their manager do?$qb$, $qb$Address the behavioral impact privately, listen for underlying strain without diagnosing it, set clear expectations, and agree support and follow-up.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the leader's workload without discussing behavior.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team to be patient because the leader is under pressure.$qb$, $qb$Wait for performance results to decline before intervening.$qb$, $qb$Publicly challenge the leader's tone to reassure employees.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your organization is entering a field where your expertise is dated, yet colleagues defer to you because of your title. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Make your knowledge limits visible, bring in credible expertise, learn enough to challenge assumptions, and model changing your view when evidence warrants it.$qb$, $qb$Delegate all technical decisions to the newest specialists.$qb$, $qb$Take an executive course before allowing major decisions.$qb$, $qb$Continue leading from experience because strategic judgement matters more.$qb$, $qb$Avoid expressing views so experts are not influenced by hierarchy.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An emerging customer segment fits no business unit's targets, but early evidence suggests substantial future value. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Sponsor a bounded cross-unit exploration with learning goals, ownership and decision gates, then use evidence to determine where it should scale.$qb$, $qb$Assign it to the unit with the closest existing customers.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the segment is large enough for a formal business case.$qb$, $qb$Ask Strategy to study it for the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Let interested teams pursue it independently with discretionary budgets.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A board metric you own was reported incorrectly because an acquired unit used a different definition; the error favors your performance. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the figure promptly, explain the definition mismatch and impact, and establish common controls before the next reporting cycle.$qb$, $qb$Take a practical partial step and escalate the remaining uncertainty to a higher authority.$qb$, $qb$Apply the existing policy more carefully, even though it does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects the most visible short-term target.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the conflicting evidence and continue with the established position.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A prolonged operational crisis is entering its third week. The original response team knows the situation best but is visibly fatigued. What is the strongest decision?$qb$, $qb$Move to sustainable incident command with planned rotations, structured handovers and protected expert continuity.$qb$, $qb$Act on the strongest available signal while keeping a later review point.$qb$, $qb$Seek broader consensus before making a limited decision.$qb$, $qb$Use a familiar precedent even though important conditions differ.$qb$, $qb$Choose the easiest path to implement regardless of longer-term consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A senior colleague becomes unusually combative after losing responsibility in a reorganization and is damaging collaboration. What should their leader do?$qb$, $qb$Address observed behavior and impact directly, listen to their perspective, acknowledge legitimate loss without excusing conduct, and agree expectations and support.$qb$, $qb$Resolve the immediate pressure and document the remaining risk for later review.$qb$, $qb$Use a narrower solution that improves one dimension but leaves key dependencies unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Transfer the decision to another function without clarifying enterprise ownership.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current plan even when evidence shows its assumptions are weakening.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your organization must decide in a fast-evolving scientific field where respected experts disagree. How should you lead?$qb$, $qb$Build enough understanding to distinguish assumptions and evidence, use diverse credible expertise, seek disconfirming views, and update decisions as knowledge changes.$qb$, $qb$Introduce a sensible safeguard but leave the underlying incentives or decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Run a small corrective action focused mainly on the most visible symptom.$qb$, $qb$Ask individual managers to compensate for the problem through extra effort.$qb$, $qb$Treat the issue as unavoidable and make no material change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Several customers independently use your product for a valuable purpose it was not designed to serve. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Investigate the underlying need, test value and risks through a bounded experiment, and deliberately decide whether to create a scalable offer.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate issue with a reasonable local action, but leave the broader assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather some additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask the affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or externally forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A public commitment you made will be missed because assumptions in your own plan were unrealistic, although teams warned early. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Own the planning judgment, acknowledge the warnings, reset transparently, and strengthen how assumptions are challenged and escalated.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A crisis creates dozens of executive information requests that distract the small team solving the problem. What operating model is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Create one information and decision cadence, shield the resolution team, triage stakeholder needs, and rotate critical roles sustainably.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A high-performing executive becomes defensive whenever peers question their function. What should their leader do?$qb$, $qb$Discuss the observable pattern and impact, explore triggers, and agree specific behaviors and follow-up while preserving legitimate advocacy.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue, but leave the wider assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or external pressure forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The board expects a decision in an unfamiliar technical field where internal experts strongly disagree. What is the strongest learning approach?$qb$, $qb$Learn the concepts and uncertainties material to the decision, seek competing explanations and external evidence, and challenge assumptions without pretending expertise.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A small internal team finds a revenue opportunity outside every division's plan; customers are enthusiastic but risks are unclear. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Create a bounded, accountable experiment with hypotheses, risk limits, funding and decision gates before deciding whether and where to scale.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A business you oversee reports savings that later prove to be timing shifts rather than permanent reductions. You approved the public narrative. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the characterization, own the assurance gap, quantify what is sustainable versus deferred, and strengthen how future benefits are validated.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate concern with a sensible local action, then revisit the wider implications at the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more input and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central ownership or trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current design and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until external evidence makes the problem indisputable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A prolonged restructuring is causing decision fatigue among leaders who must still run daily operations. What should the executive team do?$qb$, $qb$Reduce avoidable decision load, establish clear cadences and delegation, rotate intense responsibilities where possible, and protect recovery without losing critical control.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A peer whose role may be eliminated becomes withdrawn and stops contributing to decisions. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Speak privately without assumptions, acknowledge the uncertainty, understand what is affecting participation, and agree how they can remain meaningfully involved while decisions proceed.$qb$, $qb$Improve the existing approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask stakeholders for additional views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term compromise that reduces visible tension without addressing the underlying mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Continue the status quo because changing it could introduce new uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic technology choice requires knowledge your leadership team lacks, while vendors present conflicting claims. What is the strongest learning response?$qb$, $qb$Identify decision-critical questions, use independent and competing expertise, run targeted tests, and build enough internal understanding to challenge vendor assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate concern with a sensible local action, then revisit the wider implications at the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more input and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central ownership or trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current design and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until external evidence makes the problem indisputable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An employee proposes a potentially valuable business model that conflicts with current channel economics. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Test the underlying customer and economic hypotheses in a bounded way, explicitly examine channel conflict, and set evidence-based gates before scaling.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A business unit misses a target after you redirected resources to an enterprise emergency. Its leader is being criticized for the miss. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Make your resource decision and its consequences explicit, ensure performance is judged in that context, and own the enterprise trade-off rather than leaving the unit to absorb blame.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$At first, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$In this case, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An extended integration has left key leaders exhausted, yet another major milestone is approaching. What should the executive sponsor do?$qb$, $qb$Reassess critical work, simplify governance, rotate or reinforce overloaded roles, and protect recovery while maintaining continuity on decisions that cannot pause.$qb$, $qb$At first, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$In this case, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A respected executive becomes unusually impatient after learning they were not selected for a larger role. Their team notices. What should their manager do?$qb$, $qb$Discuss the observed change privately, listen to their perspective, separate disappointment from expected conduct, and agree support and behavioral expectations.$qb$, $qb$In this case, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$At first, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your company must assess a new technology where internal specialists are enthusiastic but independent researchers raise credible limitations. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Understand the competing claims and evidence, commission targeted tests on decision-critical uncertainties, and update the view rather than deferring to enthusiasm or skepticism.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$At first, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$In this case, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team discovers that a service designed for enterprises is attracting small businesses through an unintended channel. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Investigate the need and economics, test whether the channel can scale without damaging the core model, and use explicit evidence gates before formal expansion.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$At first, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$In this case, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic initiative you own is late because you repeatedly allowed urgent requests to consume its scarce resources. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Own the prioritization pattern, restore credible commitments, protect capacity with clearer decision rules, and explain what you will change.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though it does not fully fit the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if costs or risks are transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A major incident is contained, but executives want the same exhausted team to lead the investigation immediately because it knows the details. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Preserve knowledge through structured handover while rotating fatigued people, separating recovery from investigation where possible, and protecting decision quality.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral decision that reduces visible tension while leaving the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
