  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R09-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An internal venture is growing quickly but uses a different risk appetite from the core business. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Define which risks can legitimately differ because of the venture model and which enterprise boundaries remain non-negotiable, with governance proportionate to consequence.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-023$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$After years of volatility, the organization has become permanently cautious and slow even as market opportunity returns. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Reset risk appetite and decision pace deliberately, retain useful resilience disciplines, remove obsolete crisis constraints and rebuild confidence through bounded, accountable action.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-024$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A retiring executive's reputation is so strong that employees continue seeking their approval instead of the successor's. What should the CEO do?$qb$, $qb$Reinforce the successor's authority publicly and privately, redefine the retiring executive's role around counsel and transfer, and stop dual leadership from becoming normalized.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-025$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your board must decide on a scientific technology whose long-term societal consequences are uncertain and may extend beyond the company's lifetime. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Integrate scientific evidence, intergenerational and ethical consequences, independent expertise, staged commitments and long-term monitoring into the decision rather than relying on near-term commercial value.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-026$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A successful internal venture is becoming large enough to reshape the company's identity and cannibalize core revenue. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate enterprise future value rather than protecting identity, preserve the venture's distinctive economics, plan cannibalization deliberately and redesign the portfolio as evidence strengthens.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-027$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategy appears supported by market data, expert consensus and customer enthusiasm, but all three could be consequences of the same temporary cycle. What should you test?$qb$, $qb$Whether the evidence streams share one cyclical cause, how the thesis performs across alternative regimes and what independent evidence would distinguish structural change from a temporary boom.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-022$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You discover that an automated reporting script you set up missed logging client transactions for two days, leading to incomplete billing records. What action do you take?$qb$, $qb$Take immediate ownership, inform affected stakeholders, fix the script bug, manually reconcile the missing billing records, and audit systems.$qb$, $qb$Fix the script bug immediately and manually add the missing billing records before anyone notices the error.$qb$, $qb$Report the script error to IT support, asking them to restore missing billing records from server backup logs.$qb$, $qb$Inform your supervisor that a software glitch caused tracking gaps and request assistance in notifying billing.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see if the finance department flags billing discrepancies during monthly audits before making manual adjustments.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-023$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An unexpected system outage occurs 1 hour before a major client deadline. Incoming messages from anxious clients are filling your inbox. How do you handle the pressure?$qb$, $qb$Maintain composure, send a clear status broadcast to clients acknowledging the issue and providing a revised timeline, and focus on recovery steps.$qb$, $qb$Respond individually to incoming client messages as fast as possible to reassure them while IT works on system recovery.$qb$, $qb$Focus entirely on assisting IT with technical recovery, deferring client messaging until system operations are restored.$qb$, $qb$Forward urgent client messages to your department manager so executive leadership can handle client communications.$qb$, $qb$Send an automated out-of-office message informing clients that technical issues are being addressed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-024$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A typically reliable colleague appears distracted, missing minor details in recent team handoffs. You suspect personal stress is impacting their work. How do you approach them?$qb$, $qb$Initiate a private, supportive check-in, express appreciation for their usual work, inquire gently how you can support, and offer temporary handoff help.$qb$, $qb$Mention the missed handoff details quietly during your next work sync and ask if they need help catching up.$qb$, $qb$Adjust their submitted handoffs yourself to maintain deliverable quality without bringing up performance issues.$qb$, $qb$Inform your team lead about the missed details so workload can be adjusted officially.$qb$, $qb$Give them space for a week, assuming their work quality will return to baseline once personal stress subsides.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-026$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During a quiet operational week with minimal incoming tasks, how do you spend your available work hours?$qb$, $qb$Identify recurring team process bottlenecks, audit internal documentation for updates, and pitch a small workflow enhancement to your lead.$qb$, $qb$Reach out to team members to ask if they need assistance with their ongoing deliverables.$qb$, $qb$Complete optional online training modules available on the company learning portal.$qb$, $qb$Organize your personal digital files and clear unread emails to prepare for upcoming workload cycles.$qb$, $qb$Remain available on team chat channels while waiting for new task assignments from your supervisor.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-068$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You discover that a tracking rule you configured incorrectly has caused several tasks to bypass review for three days. No serious error has yet been found. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Own the configuration error, stop further bypasses, identify affected tasks, inform the right stakeholders, review the exposure, and correct the rule and control gap.$qb$, $qb$Fix the rule immediately and check the most recent tasks yourself.$qb$, $qb$Ask the system administrator to investigate because the issue is technical.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the next scheduled quality audit to identify any affected tasks.$qb$, $qb$Correct the rule quietly because no serious error has occurred.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-069$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Two urgent customer issues arrive while you are finalizing a high-risk report due in an hour. All three tasks matter. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Quickly assess impact and deadlines, protect the critical report checks, communicate realistic response times, and arrange help or sequencing for the customer issues.$qb$, $qb$Stop the report and respond to the customer who appears most upset.$qb$, $qb$Try to switch rapidly between all three tasks so each receives attention.$qb$, $qb$Finish the report without replying to the customers until after the deadline.$qb$, $qb$Send the report without the final checks so you can handle the customer issues.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-070$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A usually dependable colleague becomes curt and uncooperative after responsibilities are redistributed. Their work quality remains acceptable. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Speak privately, acknowledge the change, explore what they are concerned about, and address practical issues while maintaining clear expectations for collaboration.$qb$, $qb$Tell them you have noticed the change in attitude and expect professional behavior.$qb$, $qb$Give them time and avoid discussing the issue unless work quality falls.$qb$, $qb$Ask coworkers whether they know why the colleague is upset.$qb$, $qb$Move important work away from them until their attitude improves.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-072$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that three teams independently maintain similar tracking files, causing duplicated work and inconsistent data. No improvement project has been assigned. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the overlap and impact, engage the relevant owners, and propose a practical shared approach or pilot rather than waiting for formal assignment.$qb$, $qb$Merge the files for your own team so your work becomes easier.$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager that the duplication should be addressed by management.$qb$, $qb$Create a new shared tracker and ask the other teams to start using it.$qb$, $qb$Leave the situation alone because no one has assigned you responsibility for it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-114$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You promised to coordinate an approval but forgot to follow up, and the delay now threatens a milestone. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the missed follow-up, contact the approver immediately, inform affected people, propose recovery options, and improve how you track critical dependencies.$qb$, $qb$Contact the approver urgently and avoid mentioning that you forgot.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the approver has not responded.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to take over the approval.$qb$, $qb$Wait another day because repeated follow-up may annoy the approver.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-115$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During a peak week, a senior leader asks for an urgent analysis by end of day. Your team is already handling several high-risk commitments. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the decision need and minimum useful analysis, assess current priorities, communicate the capacity trade-off, and deliver a focused answer without abandoning critical controls.$qb$, $qb$Accept the request and ask the team to work late.$qb$, $qb$Delay a lower-visibility commitment without telling the stakeholder.$qb$, $qb$Produce a quick analysis without normal quality checks.$qb$, $qb$Tell the senior leader the team is too busy.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-116$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague becomes unusually quiet after their proposal is rejected in a meeting. You need their cooperation on the next phase. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check in privately, acknowledge the disappointment without assuming how they feel, listen to their perspective, and reconnect them to meaningful next-phase contribution.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to take the rejection personally.$qb$, $qb$Give them space and avoid involving them for a few days.$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague to find out what is wrong.$qb$, $qb$Remind them that the team decision is final and they must support it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-118$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A frequently used internal guide is outdated and causes repeated questions. You are not the document owner, but you know what needs updating. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the changes with the owner, prepare a clear update or evidence, and help get the guide corrected through the proper process.$qb$, $qb$Create your own updated copy and share it with your team.$qb$, $qb$Tell coworkers the guide is outdated whenever questions arise.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the owner to notice.$qb$, $qb$Replace the official file without telling the owner.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-158$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A deliverable you own will be late because you underestimated the effort. No external dependency caused the problem. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the stakeholder as soon as the risk is clear, own the estimation error, give a realistic recovery plan, and explain what you will change in future estimates.$qb$, $qb$Tell the stakeholder the work needs more time and provide a revised date.$qb$, $qb$Work late first and mention the risk only if you still cannot recover the original deadline.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to help so you can preserve the date without discussing the estimation issue.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the deadline passes, then explain that the task turned out to be more complex than expected.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-159$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A system issue, an urgent customer request, and your scheduled deadline all need attention at the same time. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess immediate impact, communicate priorities and expected timing, work deliberately in sequence, and seek help if the total demand exceeds your capacity.$qb$, $qb$Handle the system issue first because technical incidents usually affect more people, then reassess the other work.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to take the customer request while you continue your planned deadline.$qb$, $qb$Switch frequently between all three tasks so each one shows progress.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the original deadline because it was committed before the other issues arose.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-160$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A normally engaged colleague becomes unusually quiet after receiving difficult feedback in a team meeting. You need their input later that day. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check in privately, describe what you noticed without assuming how they feel, listen if they want to talk, and agree a realistic way to obtain the needed input.$qb$, $qb$Ask privately whether they are upset and offer to postpone their input until tomorrow.$qb$, $qb$Give them space for the rest of the day and get the input from someone else.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to take the feedback personally because everyone receives criticism.$qb$, $qb$Continue asking for the input as normal because personal reactions should not affect work commitments.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-162$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You finish your assigned work earlier than expected and notice a recurring team problem that nobody currently owns. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the problem is worth solving, take a manageable first step to understand or improve it, and involve the right people before changing shared work.$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager you have spare capacity and suggest the problem as a possible next task.$qb$, $qb$Create a workaround that reduces the problem for your own work.$qb$, $qb$Use the extra time to polish work that already meets the required standard.$qb$, $qb$Wait for another assignment because taking on unassigned problems can interfere with priorities.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-204$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize you failed to follow up an action you accepted in a meeting, and another colleague is now waiting for your input. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Contact the colleague immediately, acknowledge that you missed the follow-up, provide the input or a realistic time for it, and improve your action-tracking method.$qb$, $qb$Send the input now and apologize briefly for the delay.$qb$, $qb$Complete the input before saying anything so the colleague receives a finished answer.$qb$, $qb$Explain that the meeting contained many actions and yours was easy to miss.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the colleague reminds you again because they may no longer need it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-205$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer escalation arrives near the end of a difficult day when you are tired and still have one important task to finish. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the escalation's immediate impact, communicate a realistic response time, take a brief reset if safe, and sequence it with the remaining task rather than reacting from fatigue.$qb$, $qb$Handle the escalation immediately and finish your planned task afterward, even if that means working late.$qb$, $qb$Finish the planned task first because it was already scheduled.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to take the escalation without checking whether they have the context.$qb$, $qb$Respond quickly while tired so the customer knows the issue has attention.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-206$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague becomes unusually defensive when you ask about a missed deadline. You need to understand what happened without worsening the conversation. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Keep your tone calm, describe the missed deadline without judging the person, ask what affected it, listen, and then agree the recovery and future expectation.$qb$, $qb$Ask directly why they are being defensive before discussing the deadline.$qb$, $qb$Give them time to calm down and revisit the deadline next week.$qb$, $qb$Explain the consequences of the missed deadline so they understand why you are concerned.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the behavior to their manager because defensiveness prevents useful discussion.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-208$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that new team members repeatedly struggle with the same simple setup task, but no onboarding material covers it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create a short draft guide from the recurring questions, test it with the next new starter, and involve the onboarding owner before making it standard.$qb$, $qb$Write a guide and send it to the team without waiting for anyone to ask.$qb$, $qb$Keep helping each new starter personally because the task is simple.$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager that onboarding material is missing and wait for them to assign an owner.$qb$, $qb$Leave it alone because onboarding is not part of your formal responsibilities.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-250$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague tells you that a mistake in a shared file came from a section you updated, although nobody can yet see exactly when it happened. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review your change history promptly, acknowledge your possible contribution, help correct the file, and establish the cause before deciding what prevention is needed.$qb$, $qb$Correct your section and wait for others to investigate the full cause.$qb$, $qb$Say you will accept responsibility only if the audit history proves the error was yours.$qb$, $qb$Ask the file owner to determine who caused the mistake before you spend time fixing it.$qb$, $qb$Defend your update because the file passed review after you changed it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-251$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During a busy morning, two people repeatedly ask when your work will be finished while you are trying to resolve the issue causing the delay. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Give both people a realistic update and next check-in time, then protect a focused period to resolve the issue unless new information changes the priority.$qb$, $qb$Reply to every request immediately so stakeholders remain reassured.$qb$, $qb$Ignore all messages until the issue is fully resolved.$qb$, $qb$Give an optimistic completion time so people stop asking for updates.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to handle all stakeholder communication while you work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-252$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague reacts strongly when a small part of their work is criticized in a group review. You need to continue reviewing the rest of the work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the reaction without judging it, bring the discussion back to the specific work point, keep the tone respectful, and offer a private follow-up if more conversation is needed.$qb$, $qb$Pause the review and ask the colleague to explain why the comment upset them in front of the group.$qb$, $qb$Skip all remaining feedback on their work to avoid making the situation worse.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague the criticism was minor and they should not take it personally.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to continue the review because the colleague is too emotional.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-254$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A routine team dashboard requires the same manual cleanup every week, and nobody has asked you to improve it. You know a simple change could remove most of the cleanup. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the change will not affect required outputs, build a small test, show the time saved, and involve the dashboard owner before making it part of the standard process.$qb$, $qb$Make the change in your own copy and share it if colleagues ask.$qb$, $qb$Tell the dashboard owner that the process is inefficient and wait for them to act.$qb$, $qb$Continue the cleanup because improving the dashboard is not part of your assigned work.$qb$, $qb$Change the shared dashboard immediately because the benefit is obvious.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-296$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You promise to send a colleague information by noon but realize at 11:45 that you will need another hour. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague before noon, own the delay, explain when the information will realistically arrive, and check whether the change creates a problem they need help managing.$qb$, $qb$Send a quick message at noon saying you are still working on it.$qb$, $qb$Work as fast as possible and communicate only if you miss noon by more than an hour.$qb$, $qb$Send an incomplete version at noon so you technically meet the commitment.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the colleague to ask where the information is.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-297$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An important system is slow, customers are waiting, and colleagues are asking you for updates while you investigate. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set a clear update interval, focus investigation between updates, communicate what is known without guessing, and involve additional support if the impact exceeds your capacity.$qb$, $qb$Keep responding to each person whenever they ask so nobody feels ignored.$qb$, $qb$Stop all updates until you know the root cause.$qb$, $qb$Give an estimated recovery time even though you do not yet have evidence for it.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to handle the incident because the interruptions are stressful.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-298$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague who made a visible mistake keeps apologizing repeatedly even after the issue has been corrected. You need them focused on the next task. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the mistake and correction, reassure them that the immediate issue is closed, ask what they learned, and redirect attention to the next task without minimizing the impact.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to stop apologizing because everyone makes mistakes.$qb$, $qb$Give them easier work for the rest of the day so they can regain confidence.$qb$, $qb$Keep discussing the mistake until you are sure they understand its seriousness.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the apologies and assign the next task without comment.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-300$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring team meeting has no owner for maintaining its action log, and actions are sometimes forgotten. You are not the meeting lead. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Raise the gap, propose a simple ownership rule or rotating role, and help establish it rather than silently taking permanent responsibility yourself.$qb$, $qb$Start maintaining the action log yourself every week.$qb$, $qb$Remind the meeting lead whenever an action appears to be forgotten.$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager the meeting is poorly organized and wait for them to fix it.$qb$, $qb$Do nothing because meeting administration belongs to the meeting lead.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-342$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You give a teammate incorrect information that causes them to spend an hour on unnecessary work. You discover the mistake before they mention it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell them immediately, own the incorrect information, help recover any remaining impact, and correct the source or checking step that led you to give the wrong answer.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the information has changed and redirect them to the correct work.$qb$, $qb$Wait until they finish their current work so the interruption does not waste even more time.$qb$, $qb$Correct the source and avoid mentioning your mistake unless they ask.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because only an hour was lost and the project is still on schedule.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-343$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A deadline is approaching and you notice you have started making small mistakes because you are rushing. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Slow down enough to protect critical checks, reassess what truly must be completed by the deadline, and communicate or seek help if the remaining work exceeds safe capacity.$qb$, $qb$Keep the same pace but add a final review at the end.$qb$, $qb$Work faster now so you have more time to check later.$qb$, $qb$Skip low-visibility checks and focus only on the main deliverable.$qb$, $qb$Hide the mistakes you catch so others do not become concerned about the deadline.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-344$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague seems irritated when you ask them for help, but they still agree to assist. You do not know why they are irritated. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge that they may be under pressure, ask whether the timing works or another arrangement would be better, and avoid assuming the irritation is about you.$qb$, $qb$Ask why they are annoyed with you before accepting their help.$qb$, $qb$Accept the help and ignore their mood because they agreed.$qb$, $qb$Withdraw the request immediately and find someone else.$qb$, $qb$Tell them they should say no directly instead of showing irritation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-346$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that a shared folder has become difficult to use because files are named inconsistently. No one owns folder organization. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Propose a simple naming convention, test it on a small set, get agreement from regular users, and organize future files without deleting or renaming important records blindly.$qb$, $qb$Rename all files yourself using the convention you think is clearest.$qb$, $qb$Create your own organized subfolder and use it for future work.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the folder is messy and ask someone to volunteer to fix it.$qb$, $qb$Leave it because file organization is an administrative responsibility.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-388$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You agree to coordinate a small task but assume someone else will schedule the meeting. Two days later, you discover nobody scheduled it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Own the coordination gap, schedule the meeting promptly, tell affected people about any impact, and make explicit who owns logistical actions in future.$qb$, $qb$Schedule the meeting now without discussing why it was missed.$qb$, $qb$Ask the person you thought would schedule it to do so immediately.$qb$, $qb$Explain that coordination did not necessarily include scheduling.$qb$, $qb$Wait until your manager decides who should have scheduled it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-389$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A deadline moves forward unexpectedly by one day. You cannot complete everything to the original standard in the available time. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the essential outcome and quality controls, show the trade-offs created by the new date, and agree what scope, support, or timing must change rather than silently lowering standards.$qb$, $qb$Work longer and try to preserve everything without raising concern.$qb$, $qb$Reduce detail in the least visible parts of the work and meet the new date.$qb$, $qb$Tell the requester the new date is impossible and keep the original deadline.$qb$, $qb$Submit whatever is complete at the new deadline without discussing the gaps.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-390$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague receives unexpected extra work and responds with a sarcastic comment in a meeting. You need their cooperation on the task. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge that the added work may be frustrating, clarify the priority and workload impact, and discuss what support or trade-off is realistic without reacting to the sarcasm.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague the sarcasm is unprofessional before discussing the work.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the comment and repeat the instruction.$qb$, $qb$Take the work away from them because their reaction suggests they are unwilling.$qb$, $qb$Report the comment to their manager so expectations are reinforced.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-392$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that several teammates separately maintain the same reference list, and each spends time updating it. No shared list exists. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the common need, propose one shared maintained source with clear ownership, migrate a small set first, and agree how updates will be governed.$qb$, $qb$Create a shared list yourself and tell everyone to stop using their own copies.$qb$, $qb$Keep your own list accurate and share it when colleagues ask.$qb$, $qb$Tell the manager that the team is duplicating effort and wait for direction.$qb$, $qb$Leave the lists separate because each person may prefer different information.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-434$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that a task you marked complete yesterday actually has one small unfinished action that you overlooked. Nobody else has noticed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Reopen or correct the status, complete or assign the remaining action, and tell affected people if the incorrect completion status influenced their work.$qb$, $qb$Complete the small action quietly and leave the status unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone asks about the unfinished action.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to complete it so the task can remain marked complete.$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the task is substantially finished.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-435$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Three stakeholders send urgent requests within ten minutes, each describing their issue as the top priority. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare actual impact, deadlines and dependencies, communicate the priority order and expected response times, and seek additional capacity if more than one truly requires immediate action.$qb$, $qb$Handle the request from the most senior stakeholder first.$qb$, $qb$Work on the requests in the order they arrived.$qb$, $qb$Switch between all three so everyone sees progress.$qb$, $qb$Tell all three that you will respond as soon as possible without giving timing.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-436$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate receives disappointing news about a project they worked hard on and becomes quiet just before a routine team task. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the disappointment privately, give them a chance to say what they need, and agree how to handle the routine task without assuming they cannot continue.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to take the rest of the day off.$qb$, $qb$Encourage them to focus on the next task so they do not dwell on the disappointment.$qb$, $qb$Give the task to someone else without discussing it with them.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the situation because work and emotions should be kept separate.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-438$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that a routine weekly request requires people to ask the same person for a file that could safely be stored in a shared location. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the file can be shared under existing access rules, propose an authoritative shared location and update ownership, and test whether it removes repeated requests.$qb$, $qb$Upload your copy to a shared folder and tell people where it is.$qb$, $qb$Keep requesting the file because the current owner may have reasons for controlling it.$qb$, $qb$Tell the file owner that people are wasting time and wait for them to change the process.$qb$, $qb$Create an unofficial copy that your immediate team can reuse.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-480$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You tell a stakeholder that a task is complete, then discover that you misunderstood the completion criteria. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the stakeholder promptly, explain what remains, provide a realistic completion plan, and clarify the criteria so the same misunderstanding does not recur.$qb$, $qb$Finish the remaining work quickly before correcting the stakeholder.$qb$, $qb$Tell the stakeholder that the task is technically complete but one interpretation differs.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager whether the remaining work is important enough to reopen the task.$qb$, $qb$Leave the status unchanged because the stakeholder has already been told it is complete.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-481$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A high-priority issue remains unresolved after several hours, and you notice frustration is making you repeat the same troubleshooting steps. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Pause briefly, summarize what has been tested and learned, seek a fresh perspective or change the diagnostic approach, and continue with a structured next step.$qb$, $qb$Keep working through the same checklist until something changes.$qb$, $qb$Take a long break and leave the issue for the next shift without a handover.$qb$, $qb$Escalate immediately to senior management because the issue has lasted too long.$qb$, $qb$Try random alternative fixes quickly because the structured approach has not worked.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-482$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague is unusually short with you during a busy period. Later they apologize and say they are under pressure. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Accept the apology, acknowledge the pressure without excusing repeated disrespect, and discuss any practical coordination change that could reduce friction while work remains busy.$qb$, $qb$Tell them no apology is needed because everyone gets stressed.$qb$, $qb$Accept the apology but avoid working closely with them until the busy period ends.$qb$, $qb$Explain that pressure is not an excuse and ask them to control their behavior.$qb$, $qb$Report the incident so there is a record if it happens again.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-484$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring team question is answered in an old document that is hard to find. You are asked the question several times each month. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the answer is still current, move or link it into an easy-to-find authoritative location, and tell the team where future users can find it.$qb$, $qb$Keep a ready-made response so you can answer the question faster each time.$qb$, $qb$Send the old document whenever someone asks.$qb$, $qb$Tell people they should search more carefully before asking.$qb$, $qb$Create your own unofficial FAQ and circulate it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-526$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You discover that an assumption you entered into a shared plan is outdated and another team has already used it in their work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the shared assumption, contact the affected team promptly, explain the impact, and help adjust downstream work while improving how the assumption is reviewed.$qb$, $qb$Update the shared plan and wait for the other team to notice the change.$qb$, $qb$Tell the other team the assumption has changed without mentioning that you entered it.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to inform the affected team because the impact crosses departments.$qb$, $qb$Leave it until the next scheduled plan update to avoid disrupting work twice.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-527$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer issue is escalating while your first two attempted fixes have failed. Several people are watching for progress. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Stop repeating similar fixes, summarize evidence from the failed attempts, communicate the current position, and move to the next structured diagnostic step or seek appropriate expertise.$qb$, $qb$Try the most promising failed fix once more in case conditions have changed.$qb$, $qb$Keep testing different fixes quickly so observers see continuous progress.$qb$, $qb$Tell stakeholders there is no update until a fix works.$qb$, $qb$Hand the issue to someone else because two failed attempts show you are stuck.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-528$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague who is usually confident asks you to double-check a simple task after receiving critical feedback earlier in the day. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check what they are uncertain about, provide proportionate reassurance or review, and help them reconnect the task to clear criteria rather than treating the temporary loss of confidence as incompetence.$qb$, $qb$Review the entire task carefully because their confidence is clearly affected.$qb$, $qb$Tell them they know how to do the task and should trust themselves.$qb$, $qb$Take the task over so they can recover from the difficult feedback.$qb$, $qb$Tell their manager that the feedback has affected their performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-530$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that a recurring internal deadline is missed because nobody sends the first required input until someone reminds them. The reminder is not formally anyone's job. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the dependency, propose a clear trigger and owner for initiating the cycle, and help embed it into the routine rather than becoming the permanent informal reminder yourself.$qb$, $qb$Start sending the reminder every cycle so the deadline is met.$qb$, $qb$Tell the first contributor they should remember without being prompted.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to send the reminder each time.$qb$, $qb$Keep reminding people informally because the current workaround eventually works.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-572$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize that a delay you attributed to another team was actually caused partly by information your team sent late. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the record with the affected people, acknowledge your team's contribution, and update the recovery or prevention plan using the full timeline.$qb$, $qb$Tell the other team privately that both sides contributed.$qb$, $qb$Fix your team's process without revisiting the earlier attribution.$qb$, $qb$Explain that the other team's delay was still larger.$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone else notices the timeline discrepancy.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-573$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A senior person asks for an immediate answer during an incident, but you have only partial evidence and several people are waiting for your direction. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$State what is confirmed, identify the key uncertainty, give the safest provisional direction supported by current evidence, and specify what information will trigger a change.$qb$, $qb$Give the most likely answer confidently so the team can move.$qb$, $qb$Refuse to give any direction until the evidence is complete.$qb$, $qb$Ask the senior person to decide because they requested speed.$qb$, $qb$Let each person use their own judgment until more evidence appears.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-574$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague who has just returned from leave seems overwhelmed by accumulated requests and asks which one you need first. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify your genuine priority, acknowledge the volume they are returning to, and avoid presenting everything as urgent while helping identify any dependency your request creates.$qb$, $qb$Tell them all your requests are important and let them decide the order.$qb$, $qb$Withdraw your requests entirely until they catch up.$qb$, $qb$Ask their manager to prioritize their backlog.$qb$, $qb$Tell them they should have arranged better cover before taking leave.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-576$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that colleagues repeatedly ask where to find the latest version of a frequently used file. The file owner is busy and has not addressed it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the owner's preferred authoritative location, help create a clear current-version link or index, and communicate it so the team no longer depends on repeated personal questions.$qb$, $qb$Answer each colleague with the correct link when they ask.$qb$, $qb$Move the file yourself to a location you think is easier.$qb$, $qb$Tell the owner that people are confused and wait for them to fix it.$qb$, $qb$Create a duplicate copy in your team's folder.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-618$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize that you did not tell a colleague about a change that affects work they are doing today. They have already spent time using the old information. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell them immediately, own the missed communication, help assess and recover the wasted work, and improve the change-notification step that failed.$qb$, $qb$Send the new information now and apologize for the inconvenience.$qb$, $qb$Wait until they reach a natural stopping point before telling them.$qb$, $qb$Explain that they should have checked the shared source before starting.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing if their old work can still be adapted later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-619$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are leading an urgent response when two colleagues give you conflicting advice at the same time. Both are experienced and people are waiting. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the key assumption behind each recommendation, check the fastest relevant evidence, choose a proportionate next action, and state what would make you change course.$qb$, $qb$Follow the advice of the colleague with more experience.$qb$, $qb$Choose the safer-sounding recommendation immediately.$qb$, $qb$Ask both colleagues to agree before the team takes any action.$qb$, $qb$Delay action until a manager can decide between them.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-620$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague misses a small commitment and immediately explains that they are dealing with several personal pressures. You still need the work today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the pressure without probing into private details, clarify what is realistically possible today, and agree support or a revised handoff that protects the essential work.$qb$, $qb$Tell them personal issues should not affect agreed commitments.$qb$, $qb$Take the work away from them without further discussion.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to take the day off and postpone the work.$qb$, $qb$Ask for details about the personal pressures so you can judge whether the delay is justified.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-622$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that a shared meeting room is repeatedly double-booked because two calendars are being used. No one has been assigned to fix the issue. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm which calendar should be authoritative, involve facilities or the relevant owner, and help move users to one booking source so the conflict is removed at system level.$qb$, $qb$Check both calendars before every booking and warn colleagues when you see conflicts.$qb$, $qb$Create a third combined calendar for your team.$qb$, $qb$Tell facilities that the room is often double-booked and wait for them to act.$qb$, $qb$Use another room whenever a conflict happens.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-664$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize that a reminder you promised to send was never sent, and a colleague consequently missed an internal deadline. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague and affected owner promptly, acknowledge the missed reminder, help recover the deadline impact, and replace the informal reminder with a reliable tracking mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Send the reminder now and apologize to the colleague.$qb$, $qb$Explain that the colleague was still responsible for remembering their own deadline.$qb$, $qb$Ask for the deadline to be extended without mentioning the missed reminder.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because reminders are only a courtesy.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-665$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A problem is attracting senior attention, and you notice the team is rushing to provide updates before facts are checked. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set a short verification step for material facts, communicate what is confirmed versus provisional, and maintain a regular update rhythm so pressure does not turn speed into misinformation.$qb$, $qb$Send updates quickly and correct them later if needed.$qb$, $qb$Stop all updates until the team has complete certainty.$qb$, $qb$Let only the most senior team member communicate.$qb$, $qb$Give senior leaders the most likely explanation to reduce their concern.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-666$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague reacts negatively when you reassign part of their work to help meet a deadline. They say it feels like you do not trust them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge how the reassignment may feel, explain the capacity and deadline reason, listen to their concern, and clarify that the change is about workload rather than confidence in their ability.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to take the reassignment personally.$qb$, $qb$Return all the work to them so they feel trusted.$qb$, $qb$Explain that deadlines matter more than feelings during busy periods.$qb$, $qb$Ask their manager to reassure them that their performance is fine.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-668$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that a recurring meeting starts late because participants spend the first few minutes locating the same documents. No one owns preparation. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create or propose one pre-meeting location containing the current documents, assign a simple preparation owner or routine, and test whether meetings start more effectively.$qb$, $qb$Open all the documents yourself before every meeting.$qb$, $qb$Tell participants to arrive five minutes early to find their files.$qb$, $qb$Ask the meeting chair to fix the problem.$qb$, $qb$Accept the delay because it is only a few minutes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-710$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You discover that a meeting action assigned to you was completed, but you forgot to update the tracker and others are still planning around it as unfinished. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Update the tracker immediately, notify people whose planning may have been affected, and improve your completion-update habit so status reflects reality promptly.$qb$, $qb$Update the tracker and say nothing because the work itself was completed on time.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the next status meeting to correct the record.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project coordinator to update it for you.$qb$, $qb$Leave it because people should verify status before planning dependencies.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-711$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer is waiting on a call while you investigate an issue, and silence is making them increasingly frustrated. You still do not have the answer. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Give a concise update on what has been checked, what remains unknown, and when you will update again, then return attention to investigation rather than filling the call with speculation.$qb$, $qb$Keep talking through every troubleshooting step so the customer hears progress.$qb$, $qb$Stay silent until you have the answer.$qb$, $qb$Give the customer your best guess to reduce uncertainty.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to speak to the customer while you investigate, even if they lack context.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-712$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague is disappointed that someone else was selected for an opportunity they wanted. You were involved in the selection. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the disappointment, explain the job-relevant selection criteria without becoming defensive, listen to their questions, and discuss credible development steps for future opportunities.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the selected person was simply stronger this time.$qb$, $qb$Apologize for disappointing them even though the selection was fair.$qb$, $qb$Avoid discussing the decision because they may become more upset.$qb$, $qb$Promise them the next opportunity so they remain motivated.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-714$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that several people separately track the same deadline in personal spreadsheets, and dates sometimes differ. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the authoritative deadline source, propose one shared tracker or link with clear update ownership, and help colleagues retire conflicting personal versions.$qb$, $qb$Create your own accurate spreadsheet and share it with everyone.$qb$, $qb$Tell people to check their dates more carefully.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to maintain the deadline personally.$qb$, $qb$Leave the personal trackers because people prefer different formats.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-756$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You send a stakeholder an outdated attachment by mistake and notice it ten minutes later. They have not replied yet. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Send a correction immediately, clearly identify the current attachment, ask them to disregard the old one, and check whether any material difference needs explicit explanation.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether they open or respond to the old attachment.$qb$, $qb$Send the correct attachment without mentioning the earlier one.$qb$, $qb$Delete the original message if the email system allows it and assume the issue is solved.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague whether the difference is important enough to correct.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-757$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A senior stakeholder asks for updates every fifteen minutes during an issue, but each update interrupts the person doing the diagnosis. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Agree a realistic update cadence or assign a separate communicator with accurate context, so diagnosis can continue while the stakeholder receives reliable visibility.$qb$, $qb$Provide every fifteen-minute update because the stakeholder is senior.$qb$, $qb$Stop updating until the issue is resolved.$qb$, $qb$Give very brief updates even if they contain little verified information.$qb$, $qb$Ask the stakeholder to contact the technical person directly whenever they want news.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-758$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague becomes frustrated when you ask them to redo work because requirements changed after they finished it. The change was not their fault. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge that the rework is frustrating and not caused by their performance, explain the changed requirement, and discuss how to reduce or share the additional effort.$qb$, $qb$Tell them changes are normal and the work still needs to be redone.$qb$, $qb$Apologize and do the rework yourself.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to redo it because the colleague is frustrated.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to raise the issue with whoever changed the requirement.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-760$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that a recurring report requires the same manual date adjustment every month because the template is outdated. No one has asked you to fix it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the date logic, update or propose updating the controlled template, test it for the next cycle, and involve the template owner before making it standard.$qb$, $qb$Fix the date in your own copy each month.$qb$, $qb$Update the shared template immediately without telling anyone.$qb$, $qb$Tell the owner the template is outdated and wait.$qb$, $qb$Keep adjusting it manually because the fix takes only a minute.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-802$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize that you interpreted a stakeholder's request incorrectly and your team has completed the wrong version of a small task. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the stakeholder and team promptly, own the misunderstanding, confirm the intended output, and organize the least-wasteful correction while improving how requests are confirmed.$qb$, $qb$Correct the task before telling the stakeholder.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the stakeholder's request was unclear.$qb$, $qb$Ask the stakeholder to accept the completed version if it is close enough.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because the task is small.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-803$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During an incident, a senior colleague strongly suggests a fix you believe could make the problem worse. Time is short. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$State the specific risk calmly, check the quickest relevant evidence, and propose a safer reversible step or condition for trying the fix rather than yielding to status pressure.$qb$, $qb$Follow the senior colleague because they have more experience.$qb$, $qb$Refuse the fix without offering an alternative.$qb$, $qb$Ask management to decide before anyone acts.$qb$, $qb$Try the fix immediately and reverse it if things worsen.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-804$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague who normally responds quickly has missed two messages during a difficult week. You need an answer today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Send a concise follow-up stating the genuine deadline, acknowledge they may be busy, and offer an easier response route or alternative contact without assuming why they are unavailable.$qb$, $qb$Send repeated reminders until they respond.$qb$, $qb$Tell their manager they are not responding.$qb$, $qb$Assume they are overwhelmed and remove the task from them.$qb$, $qb$Wait silently because following up may add pressure.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-806$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring request arrives through several channels, and colleagues spend time checking whether it has already been handled. No one owns intake design. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Propose one visible intake point or deduplication method, involve regular users, and test whether it removes repeated checking before making it standard.$qb$, $qb$Create your own list of handled requests.$qb$, $qb$Tell colleagues to search all channels before acting.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to appoint someone to monitor duplicates.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current channels because different requesters prefer different methods.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-848$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You promised to confirm a decision by yesterday but forgot. The decision is still needed and nobody has chased you. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Contact the waiting people now, acknowledge the missed commitment, provide or obtain the decision promptly, and improve how you track promised follow-ups.$qb$, $qb$Provide the decision now without mentioning that it was late.$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone asks because the delay may not matter.$qb$, $qb$Say you were busy if the delay is later questioned.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to send the decision so your delay is less visible.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-849$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A live issue is not improving and people begin proposing increasingly risky fixes because they are impatient. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Re-establish the known facts and risk limits, compare proposed actions by reversibility and evidence, and choose the safest useful next test rather than letting frustration raise risk appetite.$qb$, $qb$Try the boldest fix because normal steps have failed.$qb$, $qb$Stop all action until a senior expert arrives.$qb$, $qb$Let the group vote on the next fix.$qb$, $qb$Continue repeating the safest earlier step.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-850$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague gives a curt response to your request and later says they are handling an urgent personal matter. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the situation without asking for private details, clarify whether your request can wait or needs another owner, and avoid treating the curt response as a personal conflict.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to take the day off.$qb$, $qb$Say the personal matter does not excuse the tone.$qb$, $qb$Withdraw every request you have made of them.$qb$, $qb$Ask what the personal matter is so you can judge how much flexibility to give.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-852$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A weekly file is repeatedly delayed because the person who prepares it waits for someone to ask. The requirement is predictable. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Propose a recurring trigger, owner and due time for preparation so the file is produced from the process rather than dependent on reminders.$qb$, $qb$Send the reminder yourself every week.$qb$, $qb$Tell the preparer they should remember the routine.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to remind them.$qb$, $qb$Accept the delay because the file eventually arrives.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-940$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You accidentally book a courier collection for the wrong day and discover the mistake after the recipient has arranged staff to receive it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell them immediately, own the error, help find the least-disruptive correction, and fix the source that carried the wrong date.$qb$, $qb$Correct the calendar silently.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see if they notice.$qb$, $qb$Say the date changed.$qb$, $qb$Ask an administrator to handle it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-941$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Registration equipment fails as a public event begins and a queue grows.$qb$, $qb$Stabilize the queue with an approved manual or alternate check-in, assign troubleshooting separately, and keep attendees informed.$qb$, $qb$Keep everyone waiting while you repair it.$qb$, $qb$Skip identity checks.$qb$, $qb$Cancel entry until the system returns.$qb$, $qb$Let each staff member improvise.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-942$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague becomes quiet after difficult feedback, but you need their expertise in a customer-preparation session.$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the situation briefly, ask what support they need, and invite their expertise without assuming they cannot contribute.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to forget the feedback and focus.$qb$, $qb$Remove them from the session.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the change in behavior.$qb$, $qb$Ask their manager to intervene.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-944$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Separate email threads are used to book the same pool vehicle, causing double bookings.$qb$, $qb$Propose one authoritative booking method with visible availability and ownership, and help move users away from competing threads.$qb$, $qb$Keep your own booking list.$qb$, $qb$Tell people to check all threads.$qb$, $qb$Ask a manager to approve every booking.$qb$, $qb$Accept occasional conflicts.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-986$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You gave another team a deadline one day earlier than the real agreed date, causing unnecessary rush.$qb$, $qb$Correct it promptly, own the error and pressure it caused, and check whether rushed work needs recovery.$qb$, $qb$Keep the earlier date as buffer.$qb$, $qb$Correct it without mentioning the mistake.$qb$, $qb$Say it was intentionally conservative.$qb$, $qb$Wait until they finish.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-987$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Senior stakeholders send many separate messages during an incident and the response team is losing focus.$qb$, $qb$Set one update channel and cadence, consolidate questions, and protect investigators from avoidable interruption.$qb$, $qb$Answer everyone separately.$qb$, $qb$Ignore all messages.$qb$, $qb$Have the technical lead answer every message.$qb$, $qb$Send partial updates to different people.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-988$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague is upset after harsh customer criticism, but the criticism contains one useful point.$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the harsh delivery, separate it from the useful substance, and help the colleague consider the valid point without endorsing disrespect.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to ignore the customer.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the useful criticism.$qb$, $qb$Challenge the customer before discussing the point.$qb$, $qb$Take the work away.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-990$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A monthly task depends on three people remembering a sequence of handoffs and often starts late.$qb$, $qb$Create a recurring trigger with owners and handoff dates so the cycle starts from the process rather than memory.$qb$, $qb$Send reminders yourself each month.$qb$, $qb$Tell everyone to use personal reminders.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to chase the first person.$qb$, $qb$Accept occasional late starts.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2819$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You told a colleague you had sent a file, then realize it remained in your drafts and their work is now delayed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Send it immediately, acknowledge your mistake and its impact, help recover the delay, and improve your send/follow-up check.$qb$, $qb$Send it now without mentioning the mistake.$qb$, $qb$Say the email system must have delayed it.$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague to send their copy.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the colleague asks again.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2820$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A system incident occurs just before a major deadline. Team members start making several changes at once to recover faster. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Establish controlled change ownership, test one evidence-based intervention at a time where practical, and record changes so the team can learn what actually affects the incident.$qb$, $qb$Allow parallel changes because speed matters most.$qb$, $qb$Stop all changes until the deadline passes.$qb$, $qb$Let the most senior person make changes without recording them.$qb$, $qb$Restart the system repeatedly until the problem clears.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2821$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague is frustrated because a customer rejected work they spent days preparing. You need to discuss what must be redone. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the disappointment, separate effort from the customer's valid requirements, and agree the necessary rework and support without minimizing their effort.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the customer is always right and the work must be redone.$qb$, $qb$Avoid discussing rework until they feel better.$qb$, $qb$Redo the work yourself so they do not feel worse.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on how much time was wasted.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2822$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring internal deadline is missed because everyone assumes someone else will start the process. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Define a clear initiating owner and trigger, document the first handoff, and embed it in the routine so the cycle does not depend on assumption.$qb$, $qb$Volunteer to remind everyone each cycle.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to send the first message every time.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team to be more responsible.$qb$, $qb$Move the deadline later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1124$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize you gave a teammate an outdated instruction and they have already completed part of the work using it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell them immediately, own the outdated guidance, assess what work is affected, and help recover it while correcting the source you relied on.$qb$, $qb$Send the new instruction and let them work out the difference.$qb$, $qb$Wait until they finish so you do not disrupt them twice.$qb$, $qb$Explain that they should have checked the latest source themselves.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to tell them because you gave the wrong instruction.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1125$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An urgent customer problem remains unresolved, and the team has been working for hours. People begin skipping documentation to move faster. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Keep essential incident records concise but current, assign documentation if possible, and prevent fatigue-driven speed from destroying the evidence needed for safe diagnosis and handover.$qb$, $qb$Stop documentation until the problem is solved.$qb$, $qb$Require full normal documentation after every action even if it slows response heavily.$qb$, $qb$Let each person decide what is worth recording.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to accept that records may be incomplete during emergencies.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1126$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague receives disappointing performance feedback and asks you whether the manager 'has it in for them.' You do not know the manager's motives. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the colleague's reaction, avoid guessing about motives, and help them separate the specific feedback and evidence from assumptions about intent.$qb$, $qb$Agree that the manager may be biased because the feedback was disappointing.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the manager is probably only trying to help.$qb$, $qb$Ask other colleagues whether they think the manager dislikes them.$qb$, $qb$Advise them to ignore the feedback until the manager proves good intent.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1128$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A weekly coordination task repeatedly starts late because the person responsible waits for confirmation that everyone is ready. That confirmation is not actually required. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the real start condition, remove the unnecessary wait, and establish a predictable trigger so the task begins on time without informal permission.$qb$, $qb$Tell the person to send more reminders asking whether everyone is ready.$qb$, $qb$Start the task yourself every week.$qb$, $qb$Move the deadline later to allow for waiting.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to confirm readiness each week.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1170$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You promised a stakeholder a response by noon but realize at 11:50 that you cannot finish the analysis accurately. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Contact them before the deadline, explain what is complete and what remains, give a realistic revised time, and provide any safe interim information they need.$qb$, $qb$Send a rushed answer by noon.$qb$, $qb$Miss the deadline and explain afterward.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to finish the analysis without context.$qb$, $qb$Send no response until the analysis is perfect.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1171$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A critical issue occurs while your manager is unavailable. Two possible actions are allowed by procedure, but one is faster and carries more reversible risk. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare the immediate impact, reversibility and evidence, choose the proportionate authorized action, document the reasoning, and update the manager when available.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the manager because the decision is important.$qb$, $qb$Choose the faster option automatically.$qb$, $qb$Choose the slower option because it sounds safer.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to choose which action they prefer.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1172$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague who made an error says, 'I knew I would mess this up,' and appears embarrassed. The error needs correction today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Address the specific error and recovery calmly, avoid reinforcing the self-judgment, and help them identify the practical cause and next prevention step.$qb$, $qb$Reassure them they never make mistakes and do the correction yourself.$qb$, $qb$Tell them confidence is the main problem.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the error and ignore the comment.$qb$, $qb$Agree they need more training before doing similar work again.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1174$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team spends ten minutes at the start of every weekly meeting collecting figures that are already available in a live dashboard. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Propose that participants review the dashboard before or during the meeting and use the saved time for exceptions, decisions and actions that require discussion.$qb$, $qb$Keep collecting figures because it ensures everyone hears the same numbers.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the weekly meeting entirely.$qb$, $qb$Ask one person to read the dashboard figures aloud faster.$qb$, $qb$Send screenshots before the meeting but still repeat the collection.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1216$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize a calculation you sent to a colleague used the wrong exchange rate and they are preparing a proposal from it.$qb$, $qb$Contact them immediately, provide the corrected calculation and impact, own the error, and help update any dependent proposal content.$qb$, $qb$Correct your own file and wait for them to refresh it.$qb$, $qb$Send the new number without explaining why it changed.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the proposal review.$qb$, $qb$Tell them exchange rates naturally change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1217$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer escalation is worsening while one team member insists on continuing a fix that has already failed twice.$qb$, $qb$Stop repeating the same attempt, summarize what the failures show, choose the next diagnostic or reversible action, and communicate the change in approach.$qb$, $qb$Let them try once more because they know the system best.$qb$, $qb$Take over and try several different fixes quickly.$qb$, $qb$Wait for a senior expert before doing anything.$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer the team is still working without changing the approach.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1218$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague appears embarrassed after asking a question that others answered quickly. Later you need them to contribute on their area of expertise.$qb$, $qb$Treat the earlier question normally, invite their expertise genuinely on the later topic, and avoid drawing attention to their embarrassment unless they raise it.$qb$, $qb$Reassure them publicly that the question was not stupid.$qb$, $qb$Avoid asking them anything else that day.$qb$, $qb$Tell them they need more confidence.$qb$, $qb$Ask another person for the expertise to reduce pressure.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1220$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring team update requires someone to manually combine five identical-format files every Friday.$qb$, $qb$Explore an approved automated consolidation, validate it against the manual result, and establish ownership for exceptions rather than repeating the merge indefinitely.$qb$, $qb$Assign the merge to the fastest employee.$qb$, $qb$Create a detailed merging checklist.$qb$, $qb$Ask contributors to send one combined file somehow.$qb$, $qb$Stop producing the update.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1262$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You discover that a spreadsheet you sent contains an incorrect filter and therefore omitted several records. A colleague has already started reviewing it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague immediately, send the corrected complete file, explain what was omitted, and help determine whether any review work must be repeated.$qb$, $qb$Send the corrected file without mentioning the omission.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the colleague finishes reviewing the first file.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to add the missing records themselves.$qb$, $qb$Say the records were added later rather than admitting the filter error.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1263$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A high-pressure issue is taking longer than expected, and a senior stakeholder demands a guaranteed resolution time that the team cannot support. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain what is known, give the best evidence-based range or next diagnostic milestone, and commit to update points rather than inventing a guarantee.$qb$, $qb$Give the most likely resolution time as a firm commitment.$qb$, $qb$Refuse to discuss timing at all.$qb$, $qb$Ask the technical team to choose a time that will satisfy the stakeholder.$qb$, $qb$Promise an optimistic time and revise it later if necessary.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1264$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague is unusually quiet after being interrupted several times in a meeting. You need their technical view before a decision. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create space for them to give their view, address repeated interruption if needed, and focus on obtaining the relevant expertise without speaking for them.$qb$, $qb$Ask them privately after the decision is made.$qb$, $qb$Tell the interrupters to leave the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Assume silence means they have no concern.$qb$, $qb$Give their likely view based on previous discussions.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1266$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Every Friday, one employee manually copies attendance totals from four identical regional reports into a summary, even though the reports follow the same structure. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Test an approved consolidation method that pulls the four totals into the summary automatically, validate it against the current process, and define who handles exceptions.$qb$, $qb$Give the employee a faster copy-and-paste routine.$qb$, $qb$Rotate the copying task among the team.$qb$, $qb$Ask each region to calculate the final summary themselves.$qb$, $qb$Stop producing the summary because the source reports already exist.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1308$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize that a customer-facing table you prepared contains one outdated price and sales has already sent it to a prospect. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Alert sales immediately, provide the corrected price and affected wording, own the error, and help ensure the prospect receives a clear correction before relying on it.$qb$, $qb$Correct the internal table and wait to see if the prospect asks.$qb$, $qb$Tell sales the price has changed since the table was sent.$qb$, $qb$Ask sales to decide whether the difference is material.$qb$, $qb$Leave it because the prospect has not yet accepted the offer.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1309$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During a busy operational disruption, several small issues occur at once and the team starts treating all of them as equally urgent. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Prioritize by safety, customer and operational impact, assign ownership, and keep lower-impact issues visible without allowing them to distract from the critical path.$qb$, $qb$Work on whichever issue was reported first.$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to choose their own highest priority.$qb$, $qb$Treat every issue as critical until resolved.$qb$, $qb$Ignore small issues completely until the main disruption ends.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1310$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague becomes upset after a customer cancels work they spent a week preparing. Some of the preparation can be reused elsewhere. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the disappointment, identify what work can be reused, and help redirect effort while avoiding dismissive comments about the time already spent.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to worry because the work can be reused.$qb$, $qb$Focus immediately on finding another customer for the work.$qb$, $qb$Let them abandon all related work for the day.$qb$, $qb$Tell them cancellations are a normal part of business.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1312$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team repeatedly spends time locating the current version of a recurring presentation because copies are stored in several folders. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Establish one authoritative location and naming or version rule, move or archive competing copies, and make the current file easy to find.$qb$, $qb$Create another folder called 'Latest.'$qb$, $qb$Tell everyone to search by modified date.$qb$, $qb$Ask one employee to send the file whenever someone needs it.$qb$, $qb$Keep all copies because they provide backup.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1354$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize a figure you supplied to another team used the wrong unit, and they are already building a forecast from it.$qb$, $qb$Tell them immediately, provide the corrected figure and unit, explain the impact, and help identify calculations that need to be rerun.$qb$, $qb$Correct your source and wait for them to refresh.$qb$, $qb$Send the new figure without explaining the unit error.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the forecast review.$qb$, $qb$Say the unit convention changed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1355$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer incident has several possible causes, and the team starts changing multiple settings at once to save time.$qb$, $qb$Establish a controlled diagnostic sequence, change one meaningful variable at a time where practical, and record results so evidence is not lost under pressure.$qb$, $qb$Change everything likely to help at once.$qb$, $qb$Stop all action until the cause is certain.$qb$, $qb$Let each specialist change their own area independently.$qb$, $qb$Restart the service repeatedly.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1356$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague is disappointed after being removed from a task because priorities changed, not because of their performance.$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the disappointment, explain the priority reason clearly, and discuss where their work or skills can still contribute without implying a performance problem.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to take it personally.$qb$, $qb$Avoid discussing the reason because it may upset them.$qb$, $qb$Say their work was no longer needed.$qb$, $qb$Promise them the next high-profile task.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1358$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring meeting starts with ten minutes spent asking who owns each open action, although ownership is already stored in the tracker.$qb$, $qb$Use the tracker as the starting point and spend meeting time only on changed ownership, blockers and decisions.$qb$, $qb$Keep reading every action aloud for completeness.$qb$, $qb$Ask one person to memorize all owners.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the meeting entirely.$qb$, $qb$Create another ownership list for meetings.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1400$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that a customer update you sent names the wrong contact person, although the rest of the information is correct. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the message promptly, acknowledge the naming error appropriately, and check whether it caused any routing or confidentiality issue.$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the substantive information is correct.$qb$, $qb$Send a corrected version without mentioning the error.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the customer to point it out.$qb$, $qb$Blame the contact list for being outdated.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1401$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A major deadline is approaching and a colleague proposes skipping a backup step because the primary process has worked all day. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess what the backup step protects and the consequence of failure, preserve it if material, and find time savings in lower-risk work instead.$qb$, $qb$Skip it because the primary process is currently stable.$qb$, $qb$Keep every normal step unchanged regardless of urgency.$qb$, $qb$Ask the most senior person present whether to skip it.$qb$, $qb$Do the backup step after the deadline.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1402$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague receives a short, blunt message from a manager and assumes they are in trouble. You know the manager often writes briefly when busy. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the colleague's concern, avoid claiming to know the manager's intent, and help them focus on the actual request or seek clarification if needed.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to worry because the manager always writes that way.$qb$, $qb$Agree that the message sounds angry.$qb$, $qb$Ask other colleagues whether the manager is upset.$qb$, $qb$Advise them to apologize immediately.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1404$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A monthly review begins with someone manually reading out dates already visible to everyone on the shared calendar. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Propose using the calendar as the baseline and discuss only changes, conflicts or decisions, freeing meeting time for useful coordination.$qb$, $qb$Keep reading dates aloud to ensure everyone notices them.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the monthly review.$qb$, $qb$Ask people to read the dates faster.$qb$, $qb$Create a separate date summary before the meeting.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1446$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize a date you gave another team was wrong and they have already arranged work around it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the other team immediately, correct the date, explain the impact, and help adjust work already arranged around your error.$qb$, $qb$Change the date silently in the shared plan.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether their work is affected.$qb$, $qb$Say the schedule changed rather than admitting the error.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to notify them.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1447$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During an urgent outage, several people start investigating the same symptom while another critical symptom has no owner. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assign owners by impact and diagnostic need so the unowned critical symptom is covered and duplicate investigation is reduced.$qb$, $qb$Let everyone continue investigating what they noticed first.$qb$, $qb$Tell the most senior person to investigate everything.$qb$, $qb$Stop all work until one cause is agreed.$qb$, $qb$Ignore smaller symptoms entirely.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1448$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague is upset because work they prepared was cancelled after a business-priority change, not because of poor performance. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the disappointment, explain the priority change clearly, and identify reusable work or another meaningful contribution without implying poor performance.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to take it personally.$qb$, $qb$Avoid explaining why they were removed.$qb$, $qb$Promise them the next important task.$qb$, $qb$Let them abandon the prepared work completely.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1450$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team spends time each week manually reminding owners about actions already showing overdue alerts in the shared system. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the system's overdue alerts as the default and spend human effort only on exceptions or escalations the alerts do not resolve.$qb$, $qb$Keep manual reminders because they are more personal.$qb$, $qb$Rotate reminder duty.$qb$, $qb$Send more reminders to ensure action.$qb$, $qb$Turn off the system alerts and keep the manual process.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1492$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You discover that a meeting invitation you sent gives the wrong building, and several external attendees may already be travelling. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Contact attendees immediately through the available channels, give the correct location and practical recovery information, own the error, and update the invitation.$qb$, $qb$Correct the calendar invitation and assume everyone will see it.$qb$, $qb$Wait for attendees to call if they cannot find the room.$qb$, $qb$Say the venue changed at short notice.$qb$, $qb$Ask reception to redirect anyone who arrives at the wrong building.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1493$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A high-pressure customer issue is unresolved and the team starts extending working hours without deciding who must remain involved. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the critical roles needed for the next phase, rotate or release others, set handover points, and preserve effective coverage rather than keeping everyone working from anxiety.$qb$, $qb$Keep everyone available until the issue is resolved.$qb$, $qb$Send everyone home and resume tomorrow.$qb$, $qb$Let people decide individually when to stop.$qb$, $qb$Ask only the most senior employees to stay.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1494$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague becomes frustrated after receiving conflicting feedback from two reviewers. They ask which reviewer they should ignore. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the frustration, compare the feedback against the work objective and decision authority, and help reconcile genuine differences rather than dismissing one reviewer.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to follow the more senior reviewer.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to choose the feedback they prefer.$qb$, $qb$Ask both reviewers to rewrite the work themselves.$qb$, $qb$Ignore both sets of feedback and keep the original.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1496$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team manually copies the same three performance numbers into several weekly update messages. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use an approved shared source or generated update so the numbers are maintained once, with ownership for commentary and exceptions.$qb$, $qb$Create a copy-and-paste template.$qb$, $qb$Ask one person to send all updates.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the number of updates without checking stakeholder need.$qb$, $qb$Keep manual copying because each message has a different audience.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1538$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You discover that a file you sent to a customer contains an old internal comment that should have been removed. The comment is not confidential but may confuse them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Contact the customer promptly, send a clean version, explain the correction appropriately, and improve the final-document check that missed the comment.$qb$, $qb$Send a clean version without mentioning the change.$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the comment is not confidential.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether the customer asks about it.$qb$, $qb$Say the comment was intentionally included for context.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1539$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A deadline is close and the team is debating a minor issue while a major unresolved dependency has no clear owner. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Refocus the team on impact, assign ownership for the major dependency, and time-box or defer the minor issue unless it blocks the critical path.$qb$, $qb$Let the debate finish so everyone feels heard.$qb$, $qb$Ask the most senior person to solve both issues.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the minor issue completely and never revisit it.$qb$, $qb$Work on whichever issue is easiest to close.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1540$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague is upset because their work was heavily edited by a reviewer. Some edits are necessary, while others are style preferences. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the reaction, separate required changes from preferences, and help them focus on the substantive improvements without treating every edit as a failure.$qb$, $qb$Tell them reviewers always have the final say.$qb$, $qb$Reject all style edits to protect the colleague.$qb$, $qb$Redo the document yourself.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to be emotionally attached to their work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1542$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team spends time every month recreating the same chart from data already stored in a standard table. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create an approved reusable chart or automated report linked to the table, validate it, and leave manual work for commentary or exceptions.$qb$, $qb$Save last month's chart and change the labels.$qb$, $qb$Rotate chart creation among the team.$qb$, $qb$Stop using the chart.$qb$, $qb$Ask one specialist to create it every month.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1584$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize you sent a draft marked 'final' to an internal stakeholder even though one section still contains placeholder text. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Notify them promptly, send the corrected version with clear version identification, own the mistake, and improve the final-file check.$qb$, $qb$Send the corrected file without mentioning the earlier problem.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether they notice the placeholder.$qb$, $qb$Say the placeholder was intentionally left for discussion.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to ignore the section until later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1585$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer deadline is close and the team is spending time polishing a presentation while one key data point is still unverified. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Prioritize verification of the decision-critical data, freeze non-essential polish, and resume presentation improvements only after the core evidence is reliable.$qb$, $qb$Keep polishing because the presentation must look professional.$qb$, $qb$Use the unverified data with a footnote.$qb$, $qb$Remove the data point entirely.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer which matters more: accuracy or presentation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1586$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague receives feedback that their work is 'too detailed' and feels the reviewer dislikes their style. The actual issue is that decision-makers cannot find the recommendation. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the reaction, translate the feedback into the specific audience problem, and help them keep necessary detail while making the recommendation easier to find.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to cut the document in half.$qb$, $qb$Agree the reviewer probably prefers a different style.$qb$, $qb$Remove all supporting detail.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to take feedback personally.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1588$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring update requires someone to manually copy the same deadline dates from the project plan into an email every Monday. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use an approved shared view or generated update from the project plan so dates are maintained once, with manual commentary only where needed.$qb$, $qb$Create an email template and keep copying dates.$qb$, $qb$Rotate the task weekly.$qb$, $qb$Stop sending the update.$qb$, $qb$Ask recipients to open the project plan without checking whether they need commentary.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1630$qb$, $qb$A6-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that a calendar invitation you created sends visitors to the wrong campus entrance, and the meeting begins in less than an hour. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Notify the stakeholder, send a corrected clearly versioned file, own the mistake, and improve the final-file check.$qb$, $qb$Send the corrected file without mentioning the issue.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether they notice.$qb$, $qb$Say the placeholder was intentional.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to ignore that section.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1631$qb$, $qb$A6-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A service disruption is drawing attention to several symptoms. Most specialists cluster around the most visible symptom while a less visible issue could stop recovery. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Freeze non-essential polish, verify the decision-critical data first, then resume presentation improvements.$qb$, $qb$Keep polishing because presentation quality matters.$qb$, $qb$Use the unverified data with a footnote.$qb$, $qb$Remove the data point.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer whether accuracy or appearance matters more.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1632$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate has spent days preparing analysis that is no longer needed because leadership changed direction. They are visibly disappointed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the reaction and help retain necessary detail while making the recommendation and decision path easier to find.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to cut the document in half.$qb$, $qb$Agree the reviewer simply dislikes their style.$qb$, $qb$Remove all supporting detail.$qb$, $qb$Tell them not to take feedback personally.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1634$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Every month, staff rebuild the same milestone graphic by copying dates from a controlled project schedule into presentation software. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use an approved shared view or generated update from the plan so dates are maintained once, adding manual commentary only where useful.$qb$, $qb$Use an email template and keep copying.$qb$, $qb$Rotate the task.$qb$, $qb$Stop sending updates.$qb$, $qb$Tell recipients to inspect the plan without checking their needs.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2138$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague is told their report is hard to scan and concludes that their analysis is poor. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate presentation from analytical quality and help them preserve strong reasoning while improving structure.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate operational requirement with a localized fix while monitoring progress.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the current approach and wait for other departments to adapt their workflows.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the situation to department heads without proposing potential solutions.$qb$, $qb$Disengage from the issue and let other stakeholders resolve the friction independently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
