  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2738$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Teams manually create the same audit-preparation tasks and evidence folders every quarter. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use an approved recurring audit-preparation workflow or template with standard tasks, owners and controlled exceptions.$qb$, $qb$Provide standardized templates and checklists for team members to execute the steps manually.$qb$, $qb$Assign one dedicated coordinator to perform all manual tasks on behalf of the team.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the frequency of the task to lower total manual operational hours.$qb$, $qb$Continue executing the manual steps individually without exploring workflow automation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2782$qb$, $qb$A6-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague is told their written updates are too cautious and responds by removing all uncertainty language from the next update. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Help them state the conclusion clearly while retaining material assumptions, confidence limits or unknowns that decision-makers need.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate operational requirement with a localized fix while monitoring progress.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the current approach and wait for other departments to adapt their workflows.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the situation to department heads without proposing potential solutions.$qb$, $qb$Disengage from the issue and let other stakeholders resolve the friction independently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2784$qb$, $qb$A6-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Every product retirement requires the same customer notices, access-removal tasks and archive steps to be created manually. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use an approved retirement workflow or template that creates the standard tasks, owners and timing while allowing product-specific exceptions.$qb$, $qb$Implement a practical interim adjustment to address immediate operational needs while monitoring results.$qb$, $qb$Maintain existing procedures temporarily until formal company-wide guidance is published.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the issue to departmental leadership without first gathering supporting data or options.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the operational discrepancy and proceed with business as usual.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-025$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project report you submitted receives significant critical feedback from a senior stakeholder regarding analytical depth and data framing. How do you react?$qb$, $qb$View the feedback as a development opportunity, schedule a quick follow-up to clarify expectations, and rebuild the report incorporating deeper analysis.$qb$, $qb$Thank the stakeholder for the feedback and make the specific line-item revisions requested in the report.$qb$, $qb$Explain to the stakeholder the time constraints under which the initial report was produced to contextualize the depth level.$qb$, $qb$Ask a senior peer to review your updated draft before resubmitting it to ensure it meets stakeholder standards.$qb$, $qb$Accept the feedback quietly and rely on established standard templates for future reports to minimize deviation risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-071$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A senior reviewer says your analysis is accurate but repeatedly too detailed for decision-makers. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Study examples of effective executive-level analysis, ask what decisions the audience needs to make, and deliberately practice concise synthesis while preserving necessary evidence.$qb$, $qb$Shorten the next report by removing several sections.$qb$, $qb$Ask the reviewer to mark exactly which paragraphs should be deleted.$qb$, $qb$Keep your current style because accuracy is more important than brevity.$qb$, $qb$Use an AI summarizer to shorten future reports without changing your own approach.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-117$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You repeat an error that you believed you had fixed after earlier feedback. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare both incidents, identify why the earlier correction did not change the underlying habit or process, seek targeted feedback, and install a stronger preventive check.$qb$, $qb$Apologize and promise to be more careful next time.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to check all similar work permanently.$qb$, $qb$Blame workload because the error happened during a busy period.$qb$, $qb$Avoid similar tasks until you have more experience.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-161$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A junior colleague shows you a faster way to complete a task, and initial results appear as accurate as your usual method. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the method, test it on suitable examples, adopt it if reliable, and acknowledge their contribution when sharing the improvement.$qb$, $qb$Try the method on your next task and keep using it if the result looks correct.$qb$, $qb$Thank the colleague but continue your established method until a manager formally recommends the change.$qb$, $qb$Use the method privately so you gain the benefit without disrupting team practice.$qb$, $qb$Dismiss it because someone with less experience may not understand risks you have already learned.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-207$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You receive the same feedback about your written updates for the second time in three months. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask for specific examples, compare them with the earlier feedback, identify the repeated pattern, practice a changed format, and seek follow-up feedback on improvement.$qb$, $qb$Change your next update based on what you remember from the feedback.$qb$, $qb$Take a writing course when a formal training opportunity appears.$qb$, $qb$Explain that different readers prefer different styles and continue your current approach.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the repeated feedback because your updates still contain the required information.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-253$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You complete a task successfully but realize you relied heavily on a colleague for a part you are expected to handle independently in future. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the knowledge gap, ask the colleague to explain the reasoning rather than simply repeat the solution, practice the task yourself, and verify that you can perform it independently next time.$qb$, $qb$Keep notes from what the colleague did and use them as a guide next time.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to remain available the next time you perform the task.$qb$, $qb$Wait for formal training because learning informally may create inconsistent practice.$qb$, $qb$Continue relying on the colleague because the work is being completed accurately.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-299$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You perform poorly on a task you expected to handle easily because the work required a skill you have not used for a long time. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the specific skill gap, refresh it through targeted practice or guidance, apply it again on a suitable task, and check whether your performance improves.$qb$, $qb$Review the task and try to do better the next time it appears.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to handle that part of future tasks because they are more current.$qb$, $qb$Wait for formal refresher training before using the skill again.$qb$, $qb$Conclude that the skill is no longer relevant to your role because you rarely use it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-345$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are asked to use a feature in a tool you know well, but you have never used that feature before. The task is due tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify what the feature must accomplish, use reliable guidance or a knowledgeable colleague to learn it, practice on a safe example, and verify the result before applying it to the task.$qb$, $qb$Try the feature directly on the task and correct any mistakes you notice.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to complete that part because they already know the feature.$qb$, $qb$Use your familiar manual method instead so the deadline is protected.$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager you cannot use the feature until you receive formal training.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-391$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague gives you a useful technique for a task, but you do not understand why it works. You can still follow the steps successfully. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the technique where appropriate, ask enough questions or study the underlying reasoning, and test your understanding so you can adapt it when conditions change.$qb$, $qb$Keep the steps in your notes and follow them exactly next time.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to check your work whenever you use the technique.$qb$, $qb$Use your older method because you understand it better.$qb$, $qb$Treat understanding as unnecessary as long as the technique produces the right result.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-437$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You complete a course on a new skill but have no immediate project requiring it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create a small realistic practice task, seek feedback on the result, and record where the skill could be applied so the learning is reinforced before it fades.$qb$, $qb$Keep the course notes and review them when a real task appears.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to assign you a project that requires the skill immediately.$qb$, $qb$Take another course on the same topic to strengthen your knowledge.$qb$, $qb$Assume the skill will return naturally when you need it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-483$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You receive a strong performance result on a task but feedback says your method would not work well at larger scale. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Understand which assumptions break at larger scale, learn or test a more scalable approach, and compare it with your current method before the next higher-volume task.$qb$, $qb$Keep your current method until you actually receive a larger task.$qb$, $qb$Ask for an example of a scalable method and copy it next time.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the feedback because the current result was successful.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone with more experience to handle future large-scale tasks.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-529$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You solve an unfamiliar problem after several attempts, but you are not sure which change actually fixed it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Reconstruct the attempts, identify the likely causal change, document what you learned, and test the understanding safely so the solution can be repeated rather than remembered as trial and error.$qb$, $qb$Save the final working version and reuse it if the problem appears again.$qb$, $qb$Ask a more experienced colleague what they think probably fixed it.$qb$, $qb$Move on because the problem is solved and further analysis has little value.$qb$, $qb$Document every attempted change without deciding which one mattered.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-575$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You learn a new method that works well on a practice example but performs poorly on your first real case. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Compare the practice and real-case conditions, identify which assumption did not transfer, adjust your understanding, and test the method again rather than abandoning or blindly repeating it.$qb$, $qb$Return to your old method because the new one failed in real work.$qb$, $qb$Try the new method again unchanged on another case.$qb$, $qb$Ask the trainer why the method does not work and wait for their answer.$qb$, $qb$Conclude that the practice example was unrealistic and ignore the training.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-621$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are shown a faster way to perform a task, but the new method feels uncomfortable because you have used your current method for years. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Understand the new method, test it on suitable work, compare quality and effort with your current approach, and adopt it if the evidence supports the change.$qb$, $qb$Use the new method only when someone else asks you to.$qb$, $qb$Keep your current method because experience has made it reliable.$qb$, $qb$Switch completely to the new method without testing because it is faster.$qb$, $qb$Ask a junior colleague to use the new method while you continue with the old one.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-667$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You receive feedback that your analysis is accurate but takes too long to produce. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review where time is spent, distinguish essential analysis from low-value effort, test a faster approach on suitable work, and compare speed without sacrificing decision quality.$qb$, $qb$Work faster next time and see whether the deadline improves.$qb$, $qb$Keep the same method because accuracy is more important than speed.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague for their template and copy their process exactly.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the amount of analysis on every task regardless of risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-713$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You make the same type of minor mistake twice despite knowing the correct procedure. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify what in your actual workflow causes the mistake, change the cue or check at that point, and verify on the next tasks that the new habit prevents recurrence.$qb$, $qb$Read the procedure again and try to be more careful.$qb$, $qb$Add a final check for every task, regardless of where the mistake occurs.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to check your work for a while.$qb$, $qb$Accept that occasional minor mistakes are unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-759$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You receive feedback that you solve problems correctly but often take too long before asking the key question. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review recent examples to identify the questions that unlocked progress, build a simple early-question habit, and test whether it shortens future problem solving without reducing quality.$qb$, $qb$Try to ask more questions earlier next time.$qb$, $qb$Ask experienced colleagues for a standard list of key questions.$qb$, $qb$Keep your current approach because correctness matters more than speed.$qb$, $qb$Escalate problems earlier instead of trying to identify the key question yourself.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-805$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A peer solves a problem using a technique you have never seen, and the result is clearly better than yours. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the reasoning, practice the technique on a comparable example, and identify when it is appropriate rather than simply copying the steps.$qb$, $qb$Save their solution and reuse it next time.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to handle similar problems for you.$qb$, $qb$Keep your own method because you understand it better.$qb$, $qb$Use their technique immediately on all similar work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
