  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1173$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You learn a shortcut that makes a task much faster, but you later discover it works only for one type of case. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the conditions where the shortcut is valid, update your working guidance, and use the full method for cases outside those conditions.$qb$, $qb$Stop using the shortcut completely.$qb$, $qb$Keep using it until an error occurs.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to decide case by case for you.$qb$, $qb$Use the shortcut for all simple-looking cases without defining the boundary.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1219$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You learn a faster analysis method but notice it gives a different result from your established method on one edge case.$qb$, $qb$Understand why the methods differ, test the edge condition against the correct business rule, and define when the faster method is valid before adopting it.$qb$, $qb$Use the established method forever.$qb$, $qb$Use the faster method because it saves time.$qb$, $qb$Average the two results.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to choose the method each time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1265$qb$, $qb$A6-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You learn a new reporting technique that produces clearer charts, but it takes much longer than your current method. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which parts improve decision quality, practice the technique to reduce effort, and use it where the added clarity justifies the time rather than adopting or rejecting it wholesale.$qb$, $qb$Use it for every report because the charts are better.$qb$, $qb$Return to the old method because it is faster.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to create all charts.$qb$, $qb$Use the new method only when senior leaders will see the report.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
